WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.920 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.860 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:18.150 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.160 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Welcome back.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.870 align:middle line:84%
This is the second lecture on
empirical models of demand.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.460 align:middle line:84%
So let me give you a little
bit of a roadmap for today.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.560 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to first talk a
little bit about the supply

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.050 align:middle line:84%
side of these static
models of competition

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.410 align:middle line:84%
that we have been
discussing, and that's

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.750 align:middle line:84%
actually quite useful even
for informing the demand side.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:50.620 align:middle line:84%
So I hope that this will become
clear once I go through this.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.990 align:middle line:84%
Then, I'll show you the
empirical results in BLP--

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.300 align:middle line:84%
Barry, Levinson, Pakes,
the seminal paper

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.790 align:middle line:84%
that popularized the
random coefficient model.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.069 align:middle line:84%
And the results
are actually quite

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:08.100 align:middle line:84%
nice to understand
how these models work

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.710 align:middle line:84%
and what the case
for these models is.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:14.970 align:middle line:84%
And then I asked you
to read Nevo's paper

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.310 align:middle line:90%
on the cereal market.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.370 align:middle line:84%
So we'll discuss
that, and then we'll

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.080 align:middle line:90%
actually stay in this industry.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.980 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk a little
bit about the debate that

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.550 align:middle line:84%
has captivated
many people in IO,

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:32.140 align:middle line:84%
which is the common
ownership hypothesis.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.450 align:middle line:90%
So let's jump in.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.550 align:middle line:90%


00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.740 align:middle line:90%
So the supply side.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.640 align:middle line:84%
So here is the
objective function

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:49.180 align:middle line:84%
of a firm that owns
potentially different products.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.140 align:middle line:84%
This set of products
here is calligraphic I,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:02:00.100 align:middle line:84%
and it maximizes a sum of these
individual level products.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.580 align:middle line:90%
So this here is the margin.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.210 align:middle line:84%
This is a quantity
for product J,

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.850 align:middle line:84%
and this is a function
of the vector of all

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.150 align:middle line:90%
the prices in the market.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:13.970 align:middle line:84%
So think of this q here as
coming out of your demand model.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.870 align:middle line:84%
So it's the vector
of market shares

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.850 align:middle line:84%
that you get out of
your demand model times

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:22.130 align:middle line:84%
the number of consumers
that are in the market.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.050 align:middle line:84%
And so then there's
some fixed cost.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.730 align:middle line:84%
The fixed cost is not going
to be relevant for what

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.020 align:middle line:90%
we're going to do today.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.690 align:middle line:84%
And later on, you'll
learn about models

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.440 align:middle line:84%
that actually allow you to get
at those fixed costs, as well.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.025 align:middle line:90%


00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:42.690 align:middle line:84%
So now, it's going to be
very useful to define what

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.730 align:middle line:90%
is called an ownership matrix.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.080 align:middle line:84%
So again, this is potentially
a multi-product firm.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.220 align:middle line:84%
The firm owns, let's
say, several brands

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.430 align:middle line:84%
of cereals in the market,
and so each row here

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.270 align:middle line:84%
tells us for that firm that
indicates that row, which

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.280 align:middle line:90%
products does it own.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.920 align:middle line:84%
So this is simply a
matrix of binary entries.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.310 align:middle line:84%
And now you can
do the following.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.550 align:middle line:84%
You can define a
new matrix, omega,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:17.540 align:middle line:84%
that multiplies this
ownership matrix

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.270 align:middle line:84%
by the derivatives
of each product

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.155 align:middle line:84%
with regard to all
prices in the market.

00:03:24.155 --> 00:03:29.080 align:middle line:90%


00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.750 align:middle line:84%
This comes out of
the demand model.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.390 align:middle line:84%
You can only construct
those derivatives

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.450 align:middle line:90%
once you have the demand model.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.200 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this
new matrix, omega.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.360 align:middle line:84%
And with this matrix
omega, you can

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.060 align:middle line:84%
write the sets of
first order conditions

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:52.090 align:middle line:84%
of all the firms in the market
as this vector of quantities

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:56.980 align:middle line:84%
minus this omega matrix times
prices minus marginal costs.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.500 align:middle line:84%
These are all vectors, and
this omega is this matrix

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.502 align:middle line:90%
that we defined here.

00:04:01.502 --> 00:04:05.980 align:middle line:84%
And this allows us to invert
those first order conditions

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.360 align:middle line:90%
to back out marginal cost.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.590 align:middle line:84%
So this is simply
a matrix operation.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:21.190 align:middle line:84%
So what this shows us is
that with demand estimates

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.700 align:middle line:84%
and a conduct model that
actually allows us to construct

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.060 align:middle line:84%
these first order
conditions, I need

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.290 align:middle line:84%
to know how firms are
actually competing.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.900 align:middle line:90%


00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.440 align:middle line:84%
As well as observed
prices, we can recover

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.810 align:middle line:90%
a vector of marginal cost.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.850 align:middle line:90%


00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:45.470 align:middle line:84%
What this also means
is that the model

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.750 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
always rationalized

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.370 align:middle line:84%
by a vector of
marginal costs and is,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.270 align:middle line:84%
therefore, not testable if
we allow the marginal cost

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.810 align:middle line:84%
to be completely flexible
and have no other way

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:00.320 align:middle line:84%
to discern whether the marginal
costs that we back out are

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.180 align:middle line:90%
good marginal cost.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.185 align:middle line:84%
So this comes back to the
exercise that Nevo is doing.

00:05:05.185 --> 00:05:08.070 align:middle line:90%


00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:12.270 align:middle line:84%
So we're always able to
rationalize the model,

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:17.535 align:middle line:84%
but we cannot, without further
assumptions, test the model.

00:05:17.535 --> 00:05:21.910 align:middle line:84%
So again, just to
reiterate the point,

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:27.060 align:middle line:84%
depending on what the true
ownership structure looks like,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.710 align:middle line:84%
or to what extent firms
internalize profits

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.130 align:middle line:84%
of competitors, which
we'll come to later today,

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.590 align:middle line:84%
will depend on how
they want to price.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.400 align:middle line:84%
And for any such
conduct assumption,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.870 align:middle line:84%
we can back out a
vector of marginal cost.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.360 align:middle line:84%
Now, what you can
alternatively do,

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.700 align:middle line:84%
instead of allowing marginal
cost to be completely flexible--

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.140 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we would call
this non-parametric

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:00.710 align:middle line:84%
because we're not
imposing any assumptions

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.320 align:middle line:90%
on those marginal costs.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:10.332 align:middle line:84%
We can specify some functional
form for the marginal cost.

00:06:10.332 --> 00:06:12.040 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, in
the case of cereals,

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.670 align:middle line:84%
we might know that this varies
with the market price of sugar,

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:21.160 align:middle line:84%
wheat, corn, and so on, or
advertising prices, shelf

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.140 align:middle line:90%
prices, and so on.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.290 align:middle line:84%
And so if I'm pretty
confident that I

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:30.070 align:middle line:84%
can write my marginal cost as
a function of these things,

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:33.235 align:middle line:84%
I might be willing
to impose this.

00:06:33.235 --> 00:06:36.990 align:middle line:90%


00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.410 align:middle line:84%
So what this means now is that
I can come from both sides,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:41.280 align:middle line:90%
so to say.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.570 align:middle line:84%
I can estimate my demand model
with a conduct assumption,

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.425 align:middle line:84%
back out the vector
of marginal cost.

00:06:50.425 --> 00:06:51.770 align:middle line:90%
This is now data.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:55.250 align:middle line:84%
For any given conduct assumption
prices and market shares,

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.750 align:middle line:90%
this here is simply data.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:02.320 align:middle line:84%
And this here is some linear
function that I propose.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.910 align:middle line:90%


00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.045 align:middle line:90%
Now, why might this be helpful?

00:07:10.045 --> 00:07:13.390 align:middle line:84%
So you have read
Nevo's paper today

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:18.370 align:middle line:84%
where he first estimated
demand, and then tested

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:22.700 align:middle line:84%
the recovered markups
against observed markups.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.850 align:middle line:84%
What you can alternatively
do, if you're fairly confident

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.720 align:middle line:84%
in your conduct assumption--
you believe that these firms are

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.440 align:middle line:84%
maximizing their own profits,
potentially accounting for these

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.750 align:middle line:90%
multi-product effects--

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:40.520 align:middle line:84%
you can estimate the demand
and the supply side jointly.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.470 align:middle line:84%
So you can-- from
this equation here,

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.815 align:middle line:84%
which again, think of this
here as data for a moment.

00:07:44.815 --> 00:07:47.630 align:middle line:90%


00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.280 align:middle line:84%
For any given demand
parameters, you

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.370 align:middle line:84%
can think of a vector of
marginal cost that you back out.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:58.740 align:middle line:84%
You could, from this,
construct additional moments.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.510 align:middle line:84%
Remember last time,
we constructed

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:06.030 align:middle line:84%
moments interacting C with
instruments on the demand side.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.100 align:middle line:84%
We can construct a
similar set of moments

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.950 align:middle line:84%
on the supply side,
typically denoted omega,

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.920 align:middle line:84%
that we, again, interact with
some instruments of things

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.770 align:middle line:90%
that are either exogenous, or--

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:21.230 align:middle line:84%
included or excluded
instruments on the supply side.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:25.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, If I estimate
everything jointly,

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.180 align:middle line:84%
the supply side will actually
also impose restrictions

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.380 align:middle line:90%
on the demand side parameters.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:37.080 align:middle line:84%
So just to give an
example, so what happens,

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.034 align:middle line:84%
for instance, if you
find that firms price

00:08:41.034 --> 00:08:42.909 align:middle line:84%
on the inelastic portion
of the demand curve?

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:45.300 align:middle line:84%
You may recover
negative marginal cost.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.490 align:middle line:84%
I mean, for many
strategic models,

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.510 align:middle line:84%
you will be recovering
negative marginal costs.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:53.800 align:middle line:84%
You have to somehow rationalize
why firms priced it low,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.520 align:middle line:84%
and then it says, if
they need some transfers

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.680 align:middle line:84%
from somewhere else to
rationalize what's happening.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.960 align:middle line:90%


00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:10.930 align:middle line:84%
I can, alternatively, impose
that marginal costs are positive

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.845 align:middle line:84%
and use the supply
side restrictions.

00:09:13.845 --> 00:09:17.800 align:middle line:84%
And that means that when I guess
a set of demand side parameters

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:20.590 align:middle line:84%
that tell me that the
marginal costs are negative,

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.960 align:middle line:84%
but I force the
marginal cost to be

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.170 align:middle line:84%
positive through a
reasonable restriction,

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:29.350 align:middle line:84%
that this will lead to a
violation of these moment

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.410 align:middle line:90%
conditions here.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:36.840 align:middle line:84%
So it leads to larger deviations
on the supply side moment

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.480 align:middle line:84%
conditions, and the supply
side moments will, therefore,

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.150 align:middle line:84%
tell your minimization
routine, hey,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.220 align:middle line:84%
the demand side
parameters might be such

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:49.120 align:middle line:84%
that I can't recover a
good set of marginal cost.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:55.210 align:middle line:84%
So this can be very helpful
to estimate both jointly.

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.940 align:middle line:84%
Now in practice, there are
not all that many papers

00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:59.320 align:middle line:90%
that actually do that.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.860 align:middle line:84%
So I would say the
majority of papers

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.360 align:middle line:84%
just estimates the
demand side separately,

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.020 align:middle line:90%
but BLP have done this.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.610 align:middle line:84%
And in part, we credit
their good estimates

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:16.150 align:middle line:84%
for the random coefficient
parameters to imposing

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.780 align:middle line:90%
these supply side restrictions.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:19.690 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:21.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this
also informative

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:25.020 align:middle line:84%
to the extent to which you're
making way too restrictive

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.310 align:middle line:90%
assumptions on your model?

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.920 align:middle line:84%
Like say you're trying to
impose positive marginal costs,

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:32.650 align:middle line:84%
and then in your
minimization of nu

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:35.040 align:middle line:84%
you're not getting the
results you're expecting.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.290 align:middle line:84%
Is that a sign that your results
may be guided by your model

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:40.780 align:middle line:90%
extensions?

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:43.430 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So that's
a great question.

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:46.450 align:middle line:84%
So, in fact, at the
very end of the lecture

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.180 align:middle line:84%
today, we'll
discuss a case where

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:53.140 align:middle line:84%
you can use supply side
restrictions as overidentifying

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:54.940 align:middle line:90%
restrictions on the model.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:59.590 align:middle line:84%
So instead of, let's say, only
estimating the demand side

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:02.170 align:middle line:84%
and being just
identified, you typically

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:06.068 align:middle line:84%
get a lot of additional
moments out of the supply side

00:11:06.068 --> 00:11:08.360 align:middle line:84%
because you've not introduced
all that many parameters.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.110 align:middle line:84%
But maybe you have the price of
wheat, and the price of corn,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.860 align:middle line:84%
and the price of advertising,
and all those things vary.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.170 align:middle line:84%
So that leads to
overidentifying restrictions.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.950 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, with those
overidentifying restrictions,

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:24.430 align:middle line:84%
you can test different
conduct models directly.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:27.695 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's right.

00:11:27.695 --> 00:11:29.795 align:middle line:90%
And this is somewhat--

00:11:29.795 --> 00:11:31.170 align:middle line:84%
I will comment on
this later on--

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.670 align:middle line:84%
it's somewhat different from
the exercise that Nevo does.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.235 align:middle line:90%
He directly compares to data.

00:11:36.235 --> 00:11:41.460 align:middle line:90%


00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:45.420 align:middle line:84%
So my comments on using the
supply side, as I just said,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:50.940 align:middle line:84%
this oftentimes introduces
many new moment conditions.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:57.060 align:middle line:84%
It's received wisdom that it
improves the precision of demand

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.870 align:middle line:84%
estimates, and especially
the random coefficients,

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.260 align:middle line:84%
again, through the logic
that I walked you through.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:07.410 align:middle line:84%
It's econometrically efficient
to estimate the demand

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.170 align:middle line:84%
and supply side
jointly if you're

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.810 align:middle line:84%
fairly confident that your
conduct assumption is right.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:16.410 align:middle line:84%
Of course, you may
not feel comfortable

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:20.370 align:middle line:84%
making the assumptions that
firms maximize their own profits

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.170 align:middle line:90%
or collude directly.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:25.488 align:middle line:84%
So that seems a less innocuous
assumption than saying,

00:12:25.488 --> 00:12:27.905 align:middle line:84%
well, consumers just pick the
best product that they like.

00:12:27.905 --> 00:12:33.160 align:middle line:90%


00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:36.340 align:middle line:84%
And again, you can use
overidentifying restrictions

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:40.090 align:middle line:84%
to test models of
conduct, and we'll

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:44.080 align:middle line:84%
have an example of this
at the end of class today.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:50.020 align:middle line:84%
So let me talk a little bit
about the results of the BLP

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:51.190 align:middle line:90%
paper.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.790 align:middle line:84%
I think they show nicely
why you would want to favor

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.530 align:middle line:90%
random coefficients model.

00:12:56.530 --> 00:13:00.920 align:middle line:84%
So they have data
from 1971 to 1990.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:04.420 align:middle line:84%
The market here
is the entire US.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:09.120 align:middle line:84%
They use list prices, so
not transaction prices.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.200 align:middle line:90%
You could bicker about this.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.770 align:middle line:84%
And an observation
here is a model year,

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:18.245 align:middle line:90%
and they have 2,217 of those.

00:13:18.245 --> 00:13:19.870 align:middle line:84%
As characteristics,
they use the number

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:24.040 align:middle line:84%
of cylinders, doors, horsepower,
length, width, weight,

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:28.210 align:middle line:84%
wheelbase, and measures
of fuel efficiency,

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:29.920 align:middle line:84%
dummies for air
conditioning, and so on.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.880 align:middle line:84%
So here are some interesting
summary statistics

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.460 align:middle line:90%
on the market.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:38.500 align:middle line:84%
So first, something
that's striking

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:44.560 align:middle line:84%
is that the number of products
just increased a lot over time.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:50.890 align:middle line:84%
In fact, there's a recent paper
by Grieco, Murry, and Yurukoglu

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:54.190 align:middle line:84%
who estimate
mark-ups for the car

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:56.110 align:middle line:90%
market over many, many years.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:59.710 align:middle line:84%
And the number of products,
as you would not be surprised,

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:01.210 align:middle line:84%
goes up even more,
and they actually

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:04.060 align:middle line:84%
find that consumer
surplus in the car market

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:08.070 align:middle line:84%
has gone up over
several decades.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.460 align:middle line:90%


00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:15.570 align:middle line:84%
Prices here go from
$8,000 to about $10,000,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.933 align:middle line:90%
they're denoted in 1983 prices.

00:14:18.933 --> 00:14:20.850 align:middle line:84%
Something that's interesting
is that the share

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:23.470 align:middle line:84%
of domestic brands
goes down over time.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:27.610 align:middle line:84%
This is mostly driven by
increasing imports from Japan.

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:31.830 align:middle line:84%
Another interesting observation
here is that the size of cars

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.860 align:middle line:84%
is going down, very contrary
to what we see today,

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:37.300 align:middle line:84%
where cars seem to
get bigger and bigger.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:40.200 align:middle line:84%
And the authors here speculate
that this might actually

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:49.480 align:middle line:84%
be driven by the first
oil price shock in 1970.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:51.270 align:middle line:90%
So these are the summary stats.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:54.280 align:middle line:84%
This is the miles per
gallon and miles per dollar.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.930 align:middle line:84%
They use miles per dollar
on the demand side.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:00.030 align:middle line:84%
Consumers will care about how
fuel efficient their car is

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:06.120 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the dollar expenses,
but producers might not directly

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:07.410 align:middle line:90%
care about that.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.380 align:middle line:84%
They will only care about
how much more costly

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.090 align:middle line:84%
is it to produce a
fuel efficient car,

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:16.565 align:middle line:84%
so they don't care how much
the price of gasoline varies.

00:15:16.565 --> 00:15:19.410 align:middle line:90%


00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:22.890 align:middle line:84%
Here are some summary
statistics where

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:25.590 align:middle line:84%
they denote different
percentiles of their variables

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.410 align:middle line:90%
with actual models.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So you see prices go from this
famously crappy Yugo from $3,000

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:39.480 align:middle line:84%
to almost $70,000,
so a factor of 20.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:44.530 align:middle line:84%
Horsepower is also
varying widely.

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.220 align:middle line:84%
Somewhat ironically,
the Gran Fury

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.800 align:middle line:84%
comes in at the lower end
of the spectrum in terms

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.510 align:middle line:90%
of horsepower per weight.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:54.540 align:middle line:84%
And you see here different
summary statistics-- a lot

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.670 align:middle line:90%
of information on cars.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:58.250 align:middle line:90%


00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:01.280 align:middle line:84%
So here is the
first table that I

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:06.520 align:middle line:84%
think is going to be useful
to dissect a bit more.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:08.830 align:middle line:84%
And in part, I want
to make a point here

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:12.580 align:middle line:84%
that might be relevant for you
practically for those of you who

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:14.830 align:middle line:84%
want to estimate
these kinds of models.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:21.990 align:middle line:84%
So you'll notice that it takes
a while to code these models up,

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.030 align:middle line:90%
and debug them, and so on.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.780 align:middle line:84%
So it is typically useful to
do what these authors are doing

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:34.380 align:middle line:84%
here is before you estimate
the big structural model to use

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.043 align:middle line:84%
this simple inversion that
you get from the logit model

00:16:37.043 --> 00:16:38.210 align:middle line:90%
without random coefficients.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:41.850 align:middle line:84%
And remember, we can write this
as a linear estimating equation

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:44.850 align:middle line:84%
and estimate it via OLS or
two stage least squares--

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:48.545 align:middle line:84%
things that you should be well
familiar with at this point.

00:16:48.545 --> 00:16:53.730 align:middle line:84%
And you can actually see how
sensitive your coefficient

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.560 align:middle line:84%
estimates are, let's
say, to the inclusion

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:57.500 align:middle line:84%
of different
instruments and so on.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.520 align:middle line:84%
So I think that's
practically something

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:04.650 align:middle line:84%
you should keep in mind that
before you code up the big model

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.952 align:middle line:84%
and try to probe
different specifications

00:17:08.952 --> 00:17:10.410 align:middle line:84%
and different kinds
of instruments,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:14.190 align:middle line:84%
you probably want to
do this Stata, whatever

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.980 align:middle line:84%
your preferred way of
doing this is, and just run

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:19.130 align:middle line:90%
simple regressions.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:20.869 align:middle line:84%
So I found this
to be very useful.

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:24.295 align:middle line:84%
And then usually, when you
go to the random coefficients

00:17:24.295 --> 00:17:26.920 align:middle line:84%
model, of course, you get very
different substitution patterns.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:28.720 align:middle line:84%
But you already have
a pretty good idea

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.810 align:middle line:84%
what the average coefficient
should look like.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:34.810 align:middle line:90%


00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:38.600 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:40.610 align:middle line:84%
So let me tell you a
little bit about what's

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:41.695 align:middle line:90%
going on on this table.

00:17:41.695 --> 00:17:44.230 align:middle line:90%


00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:48.670 align:middle line:84%
So here, they have an OLS
specification of demand--

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:51.670 align:middle line:84%
again, based on the simple logit
model inversion-- and here,

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.410 align:middle line:90%
an IV specification.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.110 align:middle line:84%
What you can see
here is something

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:01.840 align:middle line:84%
that you would expect if firms
systematically price unobserved

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.670 align:middle line:90%
product attributes.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:07.620 align:middle line:84%
Higher unobserved product
quality leads to higher prices.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:10.270 align:middle line:84%
So you would expect
the price coefficient

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:12.260 align:middle line:90%
to be biased towards 0.

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:15.730 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, once we
start using instruments,

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:17.720 align:middle line:84%
the price coefficient
becomes more negative.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:21.200 align:middle line:90%


00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:24.260 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that you
see, and this is actually

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:28.917 align:middle line:84%
of practical relevance again,
that once you have a larger

00:18:28.917 --> 00:18:31.250 align:middle line:84%
price coefficient, or a more
negative price coefficient,

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:34.633 align:middle line:84%
you have a lot
fewer elasticities

00:18:34.633 --> 00:18:36.050 align:middle line:84%
that you get out
of the model that

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:38.360 align:middle line:84%
are on the inelastic
portion of demand,

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:41.820 align:middle line:84%
which would not be consistent
with static profit maximization.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:46.130 align:middle line:90%


00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.930 align:middle line:84%
In fact, oftentimes the problem
can be even worse than that.

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:51.380 align:middle line:84%
And speaking from
my own experience,

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:55.520 align:middle line:84%
you'll oftentimes find that
elasticities are positive.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:57.560 align:middle line:90%
And then you have a problem.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.387 align:middle line:90%
You know you have a problem.

00:18:59.387 --> 00:19:01.970 align:middle line:84%
In the other case, you might not
know that you have a problem.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:03.887 align:middle line:84%
But in that case, you
know you have a problem.

00:19:03.887 --> 00:19:08.800 align:middle line:90%


00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.140 align:middle line:84%
And so the last thing
that they do here

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:13.630 align:middle line:84%
is they estimate a
very simple supply side

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:17.380 align:middle line:84%
model of marginal cost pricing
where they simply regress prices

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:18.530 align:middle line:90%
on a bunch of attributes.

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:21.820 align:middle line:84%
This is oftentimes called
a hedonic regression.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:26.380 align:middle line:84%
And to see these here
can be interpreted

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:32.480 align:middle line:84%
as elasticities, how cost vary
with different attributes.

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:34.090 align:middle line:84%
And so what is
surprising here is

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:37.870 align:middle line:84%
that they can explain a large
fraction of the variation

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.760 align:middle line:84%
with a simple model in
terms of these attributes.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.900 align:middle line:90%


00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:49.640 align:middle line:84%
So this here is
their main table.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:51.630 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to
point out a few things.

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:56.210 align:middle line:84%
So they have here the
coefficient estimates,

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:58.940 align:middle line:84%
here the standard deviations
of those coefficients.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:00.920 align:middle line:84%
So this tells us how
heterogeneous taste

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.140 align:middle line:90%
is for different attributes.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.420 align:middle line:84%
And they estimate
two different models

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.940 align:middle line:84%
which change something
on the supply side

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:12.821 align:middle line:84%
whether or not there are
scale economies in production.

00:20:12.821 --> 00:20:17.690 align:middle line:84%
And so a few things
to point out here.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.130 align:middle line:84%
They find that--
so first, they have

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:24.150 align:middle line:84%
significant estimates for all
their standard deviations.

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:27.880 align:middle line:84%
So this is typically,
again, not easy to achieve.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:32.310 align:middle line:84%
And in part, this is because
of the supply side restrictions

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:36.180 align:middle line:84%
that lead to more
precise estimates

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:37.710 align:middle line:90%
even on the demand side.

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.990 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that's
interesting here,

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:43.120 align:middle line:84%
if you look at
this, so it's here,

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.540 align:middle line:84%
this attribute,
miles per dollar,

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:49.680 align:middle line:90%
is valued negative on average.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:55.200 align:middle line:84%
So the average
consumer doesn't seem

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.070 align:middle line:90%
to like fuel efficient cars.

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:01.860 align:middle line:84%
However, notice that the
standard deviation on this

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.990 align:middle line:90%
is also very large.

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:09.430 align:middle line:84%
And so there will certainly
be some consumers that value

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.620 align:middle line:90%
miles per dollar positively.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:15.430 align:middle line:84%
So what this model
allows us to rationalize,

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:17.200 align:middle line:84%
for instance, is that
if you, let's say,

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.550 align:middle line:84%
do this for the entire US, as
they do, in much of the US,

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:22.540 align:middle line:90%
you see big cars.

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:26.100 align:middle line:84%
People seemingly don't care
much about fuel efficiency.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.640 align:middle line:84%
But then in, let's say,
other parts of the country,

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:33.730 align:middle line:84%
you see a lot of Toyota
Priuses, and Teslas, and so on.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:37.380 align:middle line:84%
And a model that doesn't
account for these heterogeneous

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:40.350 align:middle line:84%
substitution patterns
with aggregate data

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.680 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't be able to get
at this heterogeneity--

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:45.360 align:middle line:84%
that different people
have very different tastes

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:48.270 align:middle line:84%
for these attributes,
and as a result of that,

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:49.540 align:middle line:90%
substitute differently.

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:58.982 align:middle line:90%


00:21:58.982 --> 00:22:04.580 align:middle line:84%
So a few more
tables that showcase

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.820 align:middle line:84%
that they pick up pretty
good substitution patterns.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:11.990 align:middle line:84%
Here, you see a bunch
of different models

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:16.250 align:middle line:84%
that have in their estimation,
and you can roughly

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:17.870 align:middle line:84%
think of this table
as being ordered

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:20.990 align:middle line:90%
by how fancy the cars are.

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:23.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is, of course, not
a purely vertical model,

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:26.060 align:middle line:84%
as we discussed
last week, but think

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.720 align:middle line:84%
of this as these are more
expensive, more fancy cars.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:33.230 align:middle line:84%
And if you pick,
let's say, a high end

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:36.890 align:middle line:84%
car like the Lexus,
BMW, or Cadillac,

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:39.350 align:middle line:84%
you'll see that if
you change the price,

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:40.710 align:middle line:90%
these are semi-elasticities.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:43.340 align:middle line:84%
That means that how much do
market shares change when I

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:47.690 align:middle line:90%
increase my price by $1,000.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:51.680 align:middle line:84%
You see that if you
do this for the BMW,

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:53.970 align:middle line:84%
you increase the
price by $1,000,

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:58.770 align:middle line:84%
there's almost no dent in the
market share of lower end cars.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:02.495 align:middle line:84%
And most of the action is
here at the higher end models.

00:23:02.495 --> 00:23:06.114 align:middle line:90%


00:23:06.114 --> 00:23:10.260 align:middle line:84%
And so similarly for Lexus,
and Cadillac, and so on.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:13.050 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that
validates the model

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:16.620 align:middle line:84%
is that since we are
looking at the same price

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.750 align:middle line:84%
increase for our low
end and high end models,

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:21.570 align:middle line:84%
you'll find that the
response for low end models

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.950 align:middle line:84%
is much, much stronger
than for high models,

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:24.840 align:middle line:90%
as you would expect.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:29.220 align:middle line:84%
If you're buying a $70,000
car, then an extra $1,000

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:34.815 align:middle line:84%
shouldn't be as painful to you
as if you're buying the $3,300

00:23:34.815 --> 00:23:35.315 align:middle line:90%
Yugo.

00:23:35.315 --> 00:23:44.620 align:middle line:90%


00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:48.030 align:middle line:84%
So this here is actually
a quite striking table.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:55.440 align:middle line:84%
This here shows how people
substitute to the outside option

00:23:55.440 --> 00:24:00.660 align:middle line:84%
if we base these diversion
ratios on the logit model

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:05.125 align:middle line:84%
and compare that
to the BLP model.

00:24:05.125 --> 00:24:06.900 align:middle line:84%
So I'll let you stare
at this for a second

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:13.650 align:middle line:84%
and think a little bit about
what this comparison tells us,

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:18.050 align:middle line:84%
and in what sense
the BLP model might

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:19.380 align:middle line:90%
be doing a better job here.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:21.890 align:middle line:90%


00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:26.450 align:middle line:84%
So I may just say something
which is relevant here.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:32.450 align:middle line:84%
Again, think of these as ordered
in terms of how fancy they are.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.430 align:middle line:84%
These are more expensive
cars down here than up here.

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:37.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The BLP is
more capturing the fact

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.620 align:middle line:84%
that if you're going
for an expensive car,

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:42.020 align:middle line:84%
increasing price won't
induce you to move away

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:45.620 align:middle line:84%
from buying a car in general,
but logit doesn't really

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:47.618 align:middle line:90%
get after that kind of aspect.

00:24:47.618 --> 00:24:48.660 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly right.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:52.525 align:middle line:84%
So we think that, again,
cars are durable goods.

00:24:52.525 --> 00:24:56.240 align:middle line:90%


00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.490 align:middle line:84%
If I already have a car and I'm
considering buying a cheap car,

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:01.670 align:middle line:84%
I might be much
closer to the margin

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:04.700 align:middle line:84%
where I drive my car
for another year.

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:08.360 align:middle line:84%
Whereas somebody who is
buying a $90,000 car,

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:12.560 align:middle line:84%
they'll probably not switch to
the outside option if we raise

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:14.160 align:middle line:84%
the price of that
car a little bit.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:17.420 align:middle line:84%
They might instead opt
for somewhat cheaper car.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:23.170 align:middle line:84%
And you see that the logit model
cannot rationalize this at all.

00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:28.330 align:middle line:90%
So it's quite striking.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:30.460 align:middle line:84%
And so in the BLP
model, you find

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:32.680 align:middle line:84%
that lower end
consumers are much more

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:34.990 align:middle line:84%
likely to move towards
the outset option.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:45.790 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is all I wanted
to say about those results.

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:49.030 align:middle line:84%
Let me comment on the BLP
paper, and also just give you

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:52.330 align:middle line:84%
some practical leads here
on where you can find

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:53.810 align:middle line:90%
additional information on this.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:56.650 align:middle line:84%
So, you know, as I'm sure
you may have noticed,

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.340 align:middle line:84%
we spent almost two
lectures on this.

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:00.490 align:middle line:84%
This has been a very
influential paper,

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:02.680 align:middle line:84%
led to countless
empirical studies,

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:04.640 align:middle line:84%
a large methodological
literature.

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:08.890 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the bread
and butter pieces

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.870 align:middle line:84%
of what you do in empirical
IO estimating demand,

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:16.150 align:middle line:84%
and this is the standard
model to do that.

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:18.850 align:middle line:84%
The empirical results,
the papers, of course,

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.140 align:middle line:84%
mostly known for its
methodological contribution,

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:26.290 align:middle line:84%
but the empirical results make
a nice case for this model.

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:29.260 align:middle line:84%
There were many things at the
time when this paper came out

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:31.810 align:middle line:84%
that people didn't
really know about.

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:34.810 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
nonparametric identification,

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:36.730 align:middle line:84%
to what extent are
random coefficient models

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:42.290 align:middle line:84%
identified if we do not impose a
type 1 extreme value assumption?

00:26:42.290 --> 00:26:44.450 align:middle line:84%
So people have
thought about that,

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:47.420 align:middle line:84%
and they are very general
identification results

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:54.260 align:middle line:90%
for general models of this sort.

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:57.740 align:middle line:84%
People have learned a lot
about how to estimate this

00:26:57.740 --> 00:26:59.630 align:middle line:90%
those things efficiently.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And Aviv Nevo, whose paper
we discuss after this,

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.610 align:middle line:84%
he has contributed
to telling people

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:09.590 align:middle line:90%
how to estimate these models.

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:15.140 align:middle line:84%
There are now many good sources
to dive deeper into all of this.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:20.150 align:middle line:84%
So the new IO handbook
chapter just came out in 2021.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:23.060 align:middle line:84%
If you want to learn about
demand, chapter 1 and chapter 2

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:26.750 align:middle line:90%
are good sources.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:30.020 align:middle line:84%
If you're really interested
into identification,

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:33.290 align:middle line:84%
somebody wrote me a question
about the type 1 extreme value

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:35.845 align:middle line:84%
error and how
restrictive this is,

00:27:35.845 --> 00:27:37.650 align:middle line:90%
this would be the place to look.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:40.310 align:middle line:84%
These are papers that show
you how these models are

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:42.480 align:middle line:90%
non-parametrically identified.

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:45.380 align:middle line:84%
And I would probably start
with the annual reviews

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:48.355 align:middle line:84%
article, which gives an overview
of this econometrica paper.

00:27:48.355 --> 00:27:50.980 align:middle line:90%


00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:58.580 align:middle line:84%
There is also now out
out-of-the-box estimation with

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.430 align:middle line:90%
Python that you can use.

00:28:01.430 --> 00:28:03.710 align:middle line:84%
My view on this is
this is super useful,

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:07.200 align:middle line:84%
and you may want to check
your results with that.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:09.140 align:middle line:84%
But I also believe
that you should

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:10.610 align:middle line:84%
learn how to
estimate those models

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:13.970 align:middle line:84%
and do it yourself because
that's the real skill.

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:17.540 align:middle line:84%
And especially if
you stay in IO,

00:28:17.540 --> 00:28:19.580 align:middle line:84%
part of writing an
IO job market paper

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:23.180 align:middle line:84%
is to somehow push what you
can do with these models,

00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:25.730 align:middle line:84%
and show that you understand
them deeply as opposed

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:26.960 align:middle line:90%
to just apply them.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:29.040 align:middle line:90%
So this is a great resource.

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:32.840 align:middle line:84%
But, I hope that once
you come to [14.]273,

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.080 align:middle line:84%
we teach you how to
actually do those things.

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:39.000 align:middle line:90%


00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.280 align:middle line:84%
So let me then also
just say a few things

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.985 align:middle line:90%
empirically on this paper.

00:28:43.985 --> 00:28:45.580 align:middle line:90%
We had already discussed this.

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:49.200 align:middle line:84%
The paper ignores the
durable good aspect here--

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:52.398 align:middle line:84%
that people hold cars,
and that substitution

00:28:52.398 --> 00:28:54.690 align:middle line:84%
to the offset option is,
therefore, very heterogeneous,

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:59.530 align:middle line:84%
and potentially endogenous to
the counterfactual that you run.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:01.950 align:middle line:84%
Because if you change
prices in a new car market,

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:04.690 align:middle line:84%
prices in the used car
market might respond to that.

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:07.350 align:middle line:84%
And so your counterfactual
might be misspecified

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:08.340 align:middle line:90%
as a result of that.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:11.640 align:middle line:84%
But at least, because they
have the random coefficient

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.520 align:middle line:84%
on the intercept, you get much
more heterogeneous substitution

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.860 align:middle line:84%
patterns to the
outside option than you

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:18.790 align:middle line:90%
get from a simple logit model.

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:21.680 align:middle line:84%
So it captures that
to some extent.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:24.520 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's some great
papers to look at this further.

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:27.500 align:middle line:84%
There's a paper by
Gowrisankaran and Rysman

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:31.920 align:middle line:84%
that looks at people
purchasing durable goods.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:34.460 align:middle line:84%
This is, I think,
for camcorders.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:36.440 align:middle line:84%
And so they
endogenize, basically,

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:40.050 align:middle line:84%
how people update across
different generations

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:42.470 align:middle line:90%
of camcorders.

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:48.620 align:middle line:84%
There is a paper on the used car
market by Gavazza and Lizzeri

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:53.290 align:middle line:84%
which ignores the product
differentiation aspect of cars.

00:29:53.290 --> 00:29:57.515 align:middle line:84%
So cars here just basically
described by their vintage.

00:29:57.515 --> 00:30:01.480 align:middle line:90%


00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:05.155 align:middle line:84%
So thinks about the welfare
effects of the used car market.

00:30:05.155 --> 00:30:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a more recent
paper that actually puts the two

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:09.220 align:middle line:90%
things together again--

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:14.500 align:middle line:84%
so the differentiation part, and
the fact that cars are aging,

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:20.410 align:middle line:84%
and that different consumers
might hold different vintages.

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:22.790 align:middle line:84%
This is a paper that
abstracts from dealerships.

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:25.570 align:middle line:84%
This is actually
quite problematic

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:28.660 align:middle line:84%
if you think about the
US because the US has

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:34.300 align:middle line:84%
state franchise laws that
prohibit manufacturers

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:36.670 align:middle line:90%
to sell directly to consumers.

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:38.530 align:middle line:84%
And so you might
have heard about this

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:39.980 align:middle line:90%
in the context of Tesla.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:43.480 align:middle line:84%
They have been
rallying against this

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:45.940 align:middle line:84%
because they want to sell
directly to consumers,

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:48.440 align:middle line:84%
and do not have large
dealership networks.

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:52.990 align:middle line:84%
So that might be important for
local availability of brands

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:55.060 align:middle line:84%
and substitution
patterns, and could also

00:30:55.060 --> 00:31:00.600 align:middle line:84%
lead to other problems such
as double marginalization.

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:04.470 align:middle line:84%
The model ignores that car
prices are often negotiated.

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:08.170 align:middle line:84%
I think for what they're doing,
that's not too problematic.

00:31:08.170 --> 00:31:11.070 align:middle line:84%
But we know that in reality,
the transaction price

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:14.190 align:middle line:84%
might be very different
from the list price.

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:18.030 align:middle line:84%
And I think Glenn has
taught you a paper where

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:20.640 align:middle line:84%
that has very different
implications for different kinds

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:21.310 align:middle line:90%
of consumers.

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:24.090 align:middle line:84%
So to the extent that you care
about these distributional

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:26.080 align:middle line:90%
effects, that might be an issue.

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:28.560 align:middle line:84%
And lastly, it
ignores something that

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:30.960 align:middle line:84%
is very important,
and increasingly so,

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:33.380 align:middle line:84%
which is the financial
transaction that is involved

00:31:33.380 --> 00:31:34.380 align:middle line:90%
when you purchase a car.

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:38.040 align:middle line:84%
Because you're typically getting
a loan, and maybe insurance,

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:40.734 align:middle line:90%
and other kinds of add-ons.

00:31:40.734 --> 00:31:44.790 align:middle line:84%
So obviously, this
is an older paper.

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:49.120 align:middle line:84%
There are many papers that now
address all of these issues,

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:53.190 align:middle line:84%
so that's a good paper to write
if there are many gaps to fill

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:56.060 align:middle line:84%
and people are
willing to do that.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:57.390 align:middle line:90%
Any any questions?

00:31:57.390 --> 00:31:58.991 align:middle line:90%
Otherwise, I move on to Nevo.

00:31:58.991 --> 00:32:05.165 align:middle line:90%


00:32:05.165 --> 00:32:05.665 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:05.665 --> 00:32:09.110 align:middle line:90%


00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:12.010 align:middle line:84%
So the next two papers
are in the spirit

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:16.750 align:middle line:84%
of what Glenn has already
started talking about--

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Bresnahan in 1987 and
Miller and Weinberg

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:23.150 align:middle line:84%
testing different models of
conduct against each other.

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:26.560 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is
using our estimates

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:29.570 align:middle line:84%
to figure out what game is it
that firms are actually playing?

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:37.180 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's a very nice
way to use this machinery.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:41.150 align:middle line:84%
We will look at the ready
to eat cereal market.

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:42.910 align:middle line:84%
You have read Nevo's
paper, and then we'll

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:44.590 align:middle line:84%
look at Backus,
Conlon, and Sinkinson,

00:32:44.590 --> 00:32:47.000 align:middle line:84%
which talks about the
common ownership hypothesis.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:50.180 align:middle line:90%


00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:55.680 align:middle line:84%
So the question in
Nevo's paper is,

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:01.050 align:middle line:84%
why does the cereal industry
sustain such high gross margins

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:05.222 align:middle line:84%
as measured from accounting data
and the census of manufacturing?

00:33:05.222 --> 00:33:09.990 align:middle line:84%
So they seem to be these
very large margins.

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:12.780 align:middle line:84%
There are two
competing explanations.

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.630 align:middle line:84%
One is that the
firms are somehow--

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:19.110 align:middle line:84%
of which there are few-- are
colluding with each other.

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:20.640 align:middle line:90%
And the other one--

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:22.260 align:middle line:84%
and I'm sure you've
noticed this--

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:25.560 align:middle line:84%
this is a market in which there
are a lot of different products.

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:28.710 align:middle line:84%
And it could be that consumers
have very heterogeneous tastes

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:31.710 align:middle line:84%
for those products,
and somehow, firms

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:34.770 align:middle line:84%
are catering to these
very idiosyncratic tastes,

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:37.110 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, are
able to sustain

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:40.920 align:middle line:84%
high margins in conjunction
with internalizing

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:43.620 align:middle line:84%
their multi-product incentives--
the fact that they do not

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:48.000 align:middle line:84%
want to cannibalize
their own products.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:55.560 align:middle line:84%
The approach is to use demand
estimates to recover markups.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.630 align:middle line:84%
A different alternative conduct
assumptions, it should say here.

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:04.260 align:middle line:84%
So you're using only
your demand estimates,

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.060 align:middle line:84%
imposing certain
models of conduct,

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:10.110 align:middle line:84%
and then see whether those
models of conduct with which you

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:13.560 align:middle line:84%
can recover marginal costs,
and therefore compute markups,

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:15.360 align:middle line:84%
line up with the markups
that you actually

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:16.279 align:middle line:90%
observe in the data.

00:34:16.279 --> 00:34:22.489 align:middle line:90%


00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:26.840 align:middle line:84%
And he uses panel data
on demand for cereals

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:30.110 align:middle line:84%
across different
geographic markets.

00:34:30.110 --> 00:34:33.080 align:middle line:84%
So I thought I'd use
this as an opportunity

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:36.139 align:middle line:84%
to tell you about
the surprisingly

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:38.980 align:middle line:84%
entertaining origins of the
breakfast cereal market.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:42.380 align:middle line:90%


00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:46.350 align:middle line:84%
So this here is
James Caleb Jackson.

00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:50.010 align:middle line:90%
He lived in western New York.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:53.610 align:middle line:84%
He was a very
religious vegetarian,

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:58.230 align:middle line:84%
and he stumbled into something
that resembled maybe not too

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:00.220 align:middle line:84%
much the modern
breakfast cereals.

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:02.820 align:middle line:84%
So it was kind of a
hard thing to eat.

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:03.940 align:middle line:90%
Not much fun.

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:07.890 align:middle line:84%
You had to soak it overnight in
milk to make it edible at all.

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:08.900 align:middle line:90%
He called it granula.

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:14.250 align:middle line:90%


00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:17.760 align:middle line:84%
Not much later,
there's somebody else.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:21.120 align:middle line:84%
And I should have, said
James Caleb Jackson ran

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:22.740 align:middle line:90%
a sanatorium in New York.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:26.790 align:middle line:84%
So that John Harvey Kellogg
in Michigan, his family's

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:29.250 align:middle line:90%
originally from Massachusetts.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:33.210 align:middle line:84%
He was a surgeon, and he
invented something very similar

00:35:33.210 --> 00:35:36.120 align:middle line:84%
that he first wanted
to call granula.

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:37.680 align:middle line:84%
Then he got into
some legal trouble

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:38.960 align:middle line:90%
and he said, call it granola.

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:43.280 align:middle line:90%


00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:45.800 align:middle line:84%
So you compare James
Caleb Jackson, Kellogg,

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:48.080 align:middle line:84%
and you compare
granula, granola.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:52.315 align:middle line:84%
So you may already guess whose
legacy was more enduring.

00:35:52.315 --> 00:35:55.072 align:middle line:90%


00:35:55.072 --> 00:35:58.980 align:middle line:84%
So John Harvey Kellogg
had a younger brother,

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:02.550 align:middle line:84%
and he was really
onto something.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:06.140 align:middle line:84%
Because for 10 years,
they worked together

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:09.860 align:middle line:84%
and ran a company
together until they

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:14.930 align:middle line:84%
had some differences about
including sugar into cereals.

00:36:14.930 --> 00:36:17.090 align:middle line:84%
So, again, you have to
think of the old cereals

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:20.178 align:middle line:84%
as there was a
religious impetus,

00:36:20.178 --> 00:36:22.470 align:middle line:84%
and there was a whole philosophy
around eating healthy.

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll suggest, no,
let's put some sugar in it.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:26.850 align:middle line:90%
We'll make it more successful.

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:28.565 align:middle line:90%
And it did.

00:36:28.565 --> 00:36:31.980 align:middle line:90%


00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:35.880 align:middle line:84%
John Harvey Kellogg also had
a patient, Charles William,

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:38.040 align:middle line:90%
or CW Post.

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:40.020 align:middle line:84%
So the brothers also
had disagreements

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:43.080 align:middle line:84%
about the importance
of trade secrets,

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:45.990 align:middle line:84%
and John Harvey Kellogg
said, our patients should

00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:47.560 align:middle line:90%
learn how to do those things.

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:50.460 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Charles
William Post did.

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.890 align:middle line:84%
He basically copied the
recipe, called it Grape Nuts,

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:55.985 align:middle line:84%
and became a competitor
of the Kellogg company.

00:36:55.985 --> 00:36:59.270 align:middle line:90%


00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:03.540 align:middle line:84%
So little did they know, so this
is what happened to the market.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:07.160 align:middle line:84%
Now you have hundreds
of different products.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.980 align:middle line:84%
It's also known that there's
a lot of churn in the market,

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:13.310 align:middle line:84%
and that the market is sustained
with very high advertising

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:14.390 align:middle line:90%
expenditures.

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:18.318 align:middle line:84%
And it's just funny
to look at those,

00:37:18.318 --> 00:37:20.360 align:middle line:84%
but it also illustrates
a point that there's just

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:23.300 align:middle line:90%
this massive variety.

00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:26.390 align:middle line:84%
You could be, let's say,
interested in C-3PO cereals,

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:30.080 align:middle line:84%
or maybe Donkey Kong, or
maybe Donkey Kong Junior

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:34.960 align:middle line:84%
if that's not catering
precisely enough to your taste.

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:38.620 align:middle line:90%


00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:43.180 align:middle line:84%
And I should say in
1970, there was actually

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:48.700 align:middle line:84%
a complaint by the FTC that
the companies in this market

00:37:48.700 --> 00:37:52.480 align:middle line:84%
are colluding with each other
and proliferating brands

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:55.000 align:middle line:90%
to deter entry from rivals.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:59.950 align:middle line:84%
And so Schmalensee, who
teaches at the Sloan school,

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:07.160 align:middle line:84%
has a paper on this in the
Bell Journal of Economics.

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:10.180 align:middle line:90%


00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:13.840 align:middle line:84%
And so what is striking is
you see the number of products

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:17.258 align:middle line:84%
in the market, and then
you look at how many firms

00:38:17.258 --> 00:38:18.550 align:middle line:90%
are controlling those products.

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:20.180 align:middle line:90%
And it's very, very few.

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:25.150 align:middle line:84%
So with the C3 concentration
ratio, so the market

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:30.970 align:middle line:84%
share of the top three firms,
you get up to more than 70%

00:38:30.970 --> 00:38:33.712 align:middle line:90%
of the market.

00:38:33.712 --> 00:38:38.950 align:middle line:84%
So what this tells you is this
is a market where you want

00:38:38.950 --> 00:38:43.270 align:middle line:84%
to take into account these
multi-product effects--

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:46.120 align:middle line:84%
the fact that firms
will internalize

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:49.450 align:middle line:84%
how much they will rival their
own products when they introduce

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:52.830 align:middle line:90%
new products or price those.

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.350 align:middle line:84%
It would not make
much sense to not

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:58.030 align:middle line:84%
have such a model when you
have four firms and hundreds

00:38:58.030 --> 00:38:59.240 align:middle line:90%
of different products.

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:07.550 align:middle line:90%


00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:11.390 align:middle line:84%
So again, to remind you, the
exercise that Nevo engages in

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:17.910 align:middle line:90%
is he tries to--

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:22.070 align:middle line:84%
so he first measures
markups directly

00:39:22.070 --> 00:39:27.714 align:middle line:84%
in the data based off
of accounting estimates.

00:39:27.714 --> 00:39:33.755 align:middle line:84%
And the relevant estimate here
is the retail margin of 46%.

00:39:33.755 --> 00:39:35.630 align:middle line:84%
And then he does some
bounding whether or not

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:38.600 align:middle line:90%
you include advertising as a--

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:43.040 align:middle line:84%
whether advertising is a
marginal or variable cost, and--

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:46.820 align:middle line:84%
sorry, a marginal
or a fixed cost.

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:51.410 align:middle line:84%
And in the end uses
model estimated markups

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:55.310 align:middle line:84%
to compare it to those directly
observed markups in the data.

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:57.810 align:middle line:84%
So this comes from
this report here.

00:39:57.810 --> 00:40:00.570 align:middle line:84%
But he also has some data from
the census of manufacturing.

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:09.416 align:middle line:90%


00:40:09.416 --> 00:40:15.170 align:middle line:84%
So the specification
that he uses

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:19.730 align:middle line:84%
is very, very similar to what
we have discussed last week.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:24.360 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to point
out a few things here.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:28.950 align:middle line:84%
So again, you have an
indirect utility function

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:32.170 align:middle line:84%
that's linear in terms
of product attributes--

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:34.590 align:middle line:84%
these random
coefficients for product

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:38.700 align:middle line:84%
attributes, prices for
product J and firm T,

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:41.580 align:middle line:84%
and the random
coefficient for prices.

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:45.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, the one difference
here is that he decomposes

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:48.930 align:middle line:84%
the unobserved product
attributes into a fixed effect

00:40:48.930 --> 00:40:50.345 align:middle line:90%
and a deviation from that.

00:40:50.345 --> 00:40:53.860 align:middle line:90%


00:40:53.860 --> 00:40:56.490 align:middle line:84%
So I got some questions
about this fixed effect.

00:40:56.490 --> 00:41:00.740 align:middle line:84%
So why haven't people
done this before,

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:07.210 align:middle line:90%
and what is the difficulty here?

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:13.610 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, of course,
this came not too much later

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:15.090 align:middle line:90%
after the introduction of BLP.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:17.060 align:middle line:84%
So there were not
that many people

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:19.710 align:middle line:84%
that actually learned how
to estimate those models.

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:24.360 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of a perhaps
somewhat disappointing answer.

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:28.010 align:middle line:84%
But I think there's one
important point here--

00:41:28.010 --> 00:41:29.760 align:middle line:90%
or maybe two points, actually.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:34.370 align:middle line:84%
The first one is that if you
want to include these fixed

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:37.280 align:middle line:84%
effects, you're introducing
a lot of new parameters

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:38.310 align:middle line:90%
into your model.

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:42.950 align:middle line:84%
And I walked you last
week through the steps

00:41:42.950 --> 00:41:45.950 align:middle line:84%
of model estimation, where
you had the outer loop

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:47.990 align:middle line:90%
and the inner loop.

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:52.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, what is important
here is that if you include

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:55.010 align:middle line:84%
fixed effects, you
really want to make sure

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:59.120 align:middle line:84%
that in the inner loop, you're
running a regression to back out

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:00.770 align:middle line:90%
those fixed effects.

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:03.470 align:middle line:84%
And you separate the
linear parameters

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:05.330 align:middle line:90%
from the nonlinear parameters.

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:07.670 align:middle line:84%
To remind you, the
linear parameters

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:13.370 align:middle line:84%
are all the things on
observed or unobserved product

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:15.290 align:middle line:84%
attributes, in this
case, with fixed effects

00:42:15.290 --> 00:42:18.460 align:middle line:84%
that are only J specific and
do not depend on the consumer.

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:21.866 align:middle line:90%


00:42:21.866 --> 00:42:25.300 align:middle line:84%
And so, again, fixed effects
introduce a lot of parameters.

00:42:25.300 --> 00:42:28.360 align:middle line:84%
If you can just optimize over
your nonlinear parameters

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:30.040 align:middle line:84%
and then back out
the fixed effects

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:32.650 align:middle line:84%
through a regression for any
given guess of those nonlinear

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:37.310 align:middle line:84%
parameters, that's
going to help you a lot.

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:39.210 align:middle line:90%
So that's one comment on this.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:47.410 align:middle line:84%
The second comment is that it's
a little bit against the spirit

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:50.230 align:middle line:84%
of these characteristic
space models

00:42:50.230 --> 00:42:52.370 align:middle line:90%
to estimate fixed effects.

00:42:52.370 --> 00:42:53.860 align:middle line:84%
Not to say you
shouldn't do this,

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:55.280 align:middle line:90%
or that this is not a good idea.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:58.010 align:middle line:84%
This can be very useful
for identification,

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:00.430 align:middle line:84%
but you are introducing
new parameters

00:43:00.430 --> 00:43:04.140 align:middle line:84%
for each new product, which is
something that we try to avoid.

00:43:04.140 --> 00:43:10.530 align:middle line:90%


00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:13.950 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that we
had discussed last week,

00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:16.890 align:middle line:84%
but that's new relative to BLP
that I want to point out here,

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:20.400 align:middle line:84%
is that the random
coefficients are now not just--

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:22.980 align:middle line:84%
there's not just the
standard deviation

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:25.390 align:middle line:84%
of some mysterious
unobserved distribution.

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:28.590 align:middle line:84%
They're now written in terms
of demographic variables

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:30.120 align:middle line:84%
of distributions
that you actually

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.200 align:middle line:90%
observe for each market--

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:35.470 align:middle line:84%
so the distribution of
income, distribution of family

00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:37.230 align:middle line:90%
size, and so on.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:39.580 align:middle line:84%
So I'll come back to
this once more when we

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:44.660 align:middle line:90%
discuss the estimates later on.

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:48.630 align:middle line:84%
The market here is defined as
a city quarter combination.

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:52.370 align:middle line:84%
I had some questions
about market definitions.

00:43:52.370 --> 00:43:55.920 align:middle line:84%
Vat, you asked
about this, right?

00:43:55.920 --> 00:43:58.620 align:middle line:90%
That's a good question.

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:04.180 align:middle line:84%
So the question was, why
do we define it this way?

00:44:04.180 --> 00:44:09.900 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we take commuting
zones, or, let's say,

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.080 align:middle line:84%
a quarter, or a
half a year, a year?

00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:17.580 align:middle line:84%
That's typically a
difficult choice to make.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:22.740 align:middle line:84%
What could happen is that if you
don't draw the right boundaries,

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:25.560 align:middle line:84%
that firms at the
edge of your market

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:28.780 align:middle line:84%
are actually competing with
firms in other markets.

00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:31.710 align:middle line:84%
And you mis-specify the
number of competitors

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:33.660 align:middle line:90%
that they're responding to.

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:39.020 align:middle line:84%
And so there's some papers
that use clustering procedures

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:42.540 align:middle line:84%
to figure out how
to separate markets,

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:45.710 align:middle line:84%
but in general,
this is a bit more

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.770 align:middle line:84%
an art than a science to think
about how you how you designate

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:50.540 align:middle line:90%
markets.

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:52.825 align:middle line:84%
But yes, that's an
important question.

00:44:52.825 --> 00:45:00.840 align:middle line:90%


00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:04.200 align:middle line:84%
So one more issue that comes
up with including those fixed

00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:09.330 align:middle line:90%
effects is that brands are not--

00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:13.170 align:middle line:84%
or products are not changing
their attributes much over time.

00:45:13.170 --> 00:45:15.600 align:middle line:84%
Which means that those
instruments are completely

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:18.990 align:middle line:84%
absorbing the variation
that you would be using when

00:45:18.990 --> 00:45:22.950 align:middle line:90%
you build the BLP instruments.

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:25.410 align:middle line:84%
So remember, the
BLP instruments, it

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:30.145 align:middle line:84%
was this idea that if I'm
offering a very sugary product,

00:45:30.145 --> 00:45:32.520 align:middle line:84%
and I'm in a market where all
the other products are also

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:35.500 align:middle line:84%
very sugary, then I
face more competition.

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:37.625 align:middle line:84%
I'm probably going to
respond to that in my prices.

00:45:37.625 --> 00:45:41.750 align:middle line:90%


00:45:41.750 --> 00:45:43.520 align:middle line:84%
So what he does
to get around this

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:46.520 align:middle line:84%
is to introduce two
other instruments.

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:48.110 align:middle line:84%
First, the Hausman
instruments, which

00:45:48.110 --> 00:45:50.990 align:middle line:84%
we had discussed
last week, which

00:45:50.990 --> 00:45:56.360 align:middle line:84%
is basically the idea
of using average prices

00:45:56.360 --> 00:46:02.530 align:middle line:84%
in other markets for the same
firm as proxies for costs.

00:46:02.530 --> 00:46:06.280 align:middle line:84%
So I've got some questions about
this, so I just want to ask,

00:46:06.280 --> 00:46:09.430 align:middle line:84%
does somebody want
to explain again

00:46:09.430 --> 00:46:12.040 align:middle line:84%
what the idea behind those
instruments exactly is,

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:15.310 align:middle line:84%
and what are some of the
issues that could come up here?

00:46:15.310 --> 00:46:22.310 align:middle line:84%
So again, the problem
is that in many cases,

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:23.750 align:middle line:84%
typically, costs
are not observed,

00:46:23.750 --> 00:46:27.020 align:middle line:90%
or it's hard to get at those.

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:29.360 align:middle line:84%
Remember also
that-- just picture

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:32.390 align:middle line:84%
a very simple linear
supply and demand

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:34.250 align:middle line:84%
picture, and the
identification problem

00:46:34.250 --> 00:46:36.080 align:middle line:84%
that we had discussed
at the very beginning

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:39.230 align:middle line:84%
of the last lecture,
that you ideally

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:42.710 align:middle line:84%
want some cost shifters
that shift around the supply

00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:45.830 align:middle line:84%
function to identify
your demand function.

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:48.892 align:middle line:84%
Again, cost side
instruments that you

00:46:48.892 --> 00:46:50.600 align:middle line:84%
believe are excluded
from the demand side

00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:54.610 align:middle line:84%
are typically the most
convincing types of instruments.

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:58.408 align:middle line:90%


00:46:58.408 --> 00:47:02.200 align:middle line:84%
So instead of using
directly observed costs,

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:04.480 align:middle line:84%
the idea, again, behind
these Hausman instruments

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:08.590 align:middle line:84%
is to proxy for those costs with
average prices of the same firm

00:47:08.590 --> 00:47:10.750 align:middle line:90%
in other markets.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:15.460 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is, if I'm,
let's say, the only cereal

00:47:15.460 --> 00:47:18.440 align:middle line:84%
producer that uses
rice as an ingredient,

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:20.260 align:middle line:84%
and the price of
rice goes up, then

00:47:20.260 --> 00:47:23.180 align:middle line:84%
that's going to shift
my average price.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:28.510 align:middle line:84%
And to the extent that the
shock to the cost of rice

00:47:28.510 --> 00:47:33.220 align:middle line:84%
is uncorrelated to demand shocks
across different geographic

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:39.200 align:middle line:84%
regions, I can use this as
a proxy for a cost shifter.

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:43.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, this already leads
to where the error where

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:47.710 align:middle line:84%
this might go wrong
is if you have things

00:47:47.710 --> 00:47:51.640 align:middle line:84%
in those prices that
are actually correlated

00:47:51.640 --> 00:47:53.530 align:middle line:90%
across different markets.

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:56.630 align:middle line:84%
So think about
advertising campaigns.

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:58.970 align:middle line:84%
If I'm running a big
advertising campaign

00:47:58.970 --> 00:48:02.480 align:middle line:84%
that leads to more
demand for my brands,

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:05.360 align:middle line:84%
and that's going to be
potentially reflected

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:06.950 align:middle line:84%
in the prices, I
mean, to the extent

00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:08.750 align:middle line:84%
that we think that it
is a marginal cost.

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:14.120 align:middle line:84%
And then it's as if I'm
instrumenting my endogenous--

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.760 align:middle line:90%


00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:20.660 align:middle line:84%
as if I'm instrumenting
prices with demand.

00:48:20.660 --> 00:48:22.520 align:middle line:90%
That's not a good idea.

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:28.490 align:middle line:84%
So the other
instrument that he uses

00:48:28.490 --> 00:48:30.930 align:middle line:84%
are what he calls
cost side instruments.

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:33.320 align:middle line:84%
They have a somewhat
similar logic.

00:48:33.320 --> 00:48:36.320 align:middle line:84%
So he uses region
dummies to pick up

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:39.660 align:middle line:84%
things like transportation
costs, city density.

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:43.040 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is an
issue to the extent

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:46.940 align:middle line:84%
that these things might also be
picking up systematic variation

00:48:46.940 --> 00:48:50.095 align:middle line:90%
in preferences across regions.

00:48:50.095 --> 00:48:53.260 align:middle line:90%


00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:55.550 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions on this?

00:48:55.550 --> 00:48:58.290 align:middle line:84%
You have read the paper,
so you're curious.

00:48:58.290 --> 00:48:58.790 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:00.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So one of the
things that sort of--

00:49:00.950 --> 00:49:05.660 align:middle line:84%
that utility formulation is
that the price sensitivity

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:09.530 align:middle line:84%
varies across consumers, but it
doesn't vary by type of good.

00:49:09.530 --> 00:49:14.810 align:middle line:84%
And so when they present the
table with price elasticities

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:15.590 align:middle line:90%
between--

00:49:15.590 --> 00:49:17.420 align:middle line:84%
cross-price elasticity
between types

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:21.740 align:middle line:84%
of goods, that's only driven
by the type of consumer that's

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:23.070 align:middle line:90%
buying that good, right?

00:49:23.070 --> 00:49:24.890 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:26.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I guess
my question is,

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:28.670 align:middle line:84%
how reasonable of
an assumption is it

00:49:28.670 --> 00:49:33.140 align:middle line:84%
that all of the prices, cross
elasticities that we estimate

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:35.420 align:middle line:84%
are only driven by
differences in consumers,

00:49:35.420 --> 00:49:38.840 align:middle line:84%
and not in types of
goods that we're buying?

00:49:38.840 --> 00:49:41.160 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: OK that's an
interesting question.

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:44.070 align:middle line:84%
So let me try and
say a few things.

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:45.830 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, I
mean, think first

00:49:45.830 --> 00:49:50.880 align:middle line:84%
about the idea behind the
characteristic space approach

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:54.690 align:middle line:84%
is that people value
characteristics.

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:56.970 align:middle line:84%
And so if you really
believe that that's

00:49:56.970 --> 00:49:58.710 align:middle line:84%
the way you construct
your models,

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:03.510 align:middle line:84%
then you shouldn't actually
have random coefficients

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:05.920 align:middle line:90%
be product specific.

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:09.140 align:middle line:90%
So that's one answer to that.

00:50:09.140 --> 00:50:15.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, it might be that they're a
more flexible, functional form

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:20.740 align:middle line:84%
where, let's say, your arrow is
not additively separable leads

00:50:20.740 --> 00:50:23.570 align:middle line:84%
you to a specification like this
where your random coefficient is

00:50:23.570 --> 00:50:28.060 align:middle line:84%
essentially J specific because
you're multiplying either alpha

00:50:28.060 --> 00:50:32.040 align:middle line:84%
or beta with this
unobserved term.

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:34.976 align:middle line:90%


00:50:34.976 --> 00:50:39.832 align:middle line:84%
So I think you would
probably need--

00:50:39.832 --> 00:50:41.790 align:middle line:84%
I would have to think
about, you would probably

00:50:41.790 --> 00:50:45.360 align:middle line:84%
need some, again,
variation over time

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:48.690 align:middle line:84%
to estimate such a model
because you would supposedly

00:50:48.690 --> 00:50:53.970 align:middle line:84%
want to see how does the
same consumer vary attributes

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:55.150 align:middle line:90%
across different products.

00:50:55.150 --> 00:51:00.379 align:middle line:84%
And you might need microdata for
that, but I'm not exactly sure.

00:51:00.379 --> 00:51:01.212 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:51:01.212 --> 00:51:04.830 align:middle line:90%


00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:07.420 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: OK, any
other questions?

00:51:07.420 --> 00:51:16.626 align:middle line:90%


00:51:16.626 --> 00:51:22.710 align:middle line:84%
So let me briefly
talk about his table

00:51:22.710 --> 00:51:28.118 align:middle line:84%
where he estimates, again, as
a logit model, as I explained

00:51:28.118 --> 00:51:29.410 align:middle line:90%
before, and that's a good idea.

00:51:29.410 --> 00:51:31.800 align:middle line:84%
And he has a lot of
different specifications.

00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:36.040 align:middle line:84%
The first three here are
without any use of instruments.

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:39.100 align:middle line:84%
These are all instrumental
variable specifications.

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:42.190 align:middle line:84%
And I think these two
here are including

00:51:42.190 --> 00:51:44.500 align:middle line:84%
fixed effects as controls--
so product fixed effects,

00:51:44.500 --> 00:51:46.540 align:middle line:90%
whereas the first one does not.

00:51:46.540 --> 00:51:48.400 align:middle line:84%
So what you can see
is with fixed effects,

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:50.920 align:middle line:84%
you explain a lot
more of the variation.

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:54.490 align:middle line:84%
And the price coefficients
get more negative.

00:51:54.490 --> 00:51:58.270 align:middle line:84%
And even more so when you
include consumer demographics.

00:51:58.270 --> 00:52:02.800 align:middle line:84%
So this here is an
interesting instrumental

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:06.370 align:middle line:84%
variable specification
that some of you

00:52:06.370 --> 00:52:09.140 align:middle line:84%
pick this up what
this is doing--

00:52:09.140 --> 00:52:13.110 align:middle line:84%
the inclusion of brand
dummies as instruments.

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:17.058 align:middle line:90%
What is this close to in spirit?

00:52:17.058 --> 00:52:17.993 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: BLP.

00:52:17.993 --> 00:52:19.410 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: BLP
instruments, right?

00:52:19.410 --> 00:52:22.170 align:middle line:90%


00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:27.420 align:middle line:84%
Could you think of a story
where this is preferable to,

00:52:27.420 --> 00:52:30.650 align:middle line:84%
let's say, the simple
BLP instruments?

00:52:30.650 --> 00:52:32.750 align:middle line:84%
Or why would you
want to do this?

00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:34.030 align:middle line:90%
OK, so--

00:52:34.030 --> 00:52:38.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this what's
captured on, like, advertising

00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:40.460 align:middle line:90%
effects with a brand?

00:52:40.460 --> 00:52:43.340 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:52:43.340 --> 00:52:46.040 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me answer
this in two ways.

00:52:46.040 --> 00:52:48.920 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly
right to the extent

00:52:48.920 --> 00:52:51.740 align:middle line:84%
that advertising is not captured
in the utility specification,

00:52:51.740 --> 00:52:53.910 align:middle line:90%
which it isn't here.

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:59.450 align:middle line:84%
You may be worried that
the BLP instruments are--

00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:01.550 align:middle line:84%
they're picking up that
I'm responding to the fact

00:53:01.550 --> 00:53:06.080 align:middle line:84%
that the average sugar that's
in my competitor's brands

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:08.900 align:middle line:84%
is very high-- that I'm
responding to that in some form,

00:53:08.900 --> 00:53:11.450 align:middle line:90%
or the mushiness.

00:53:11.450 --> 00:53:16.430 align:middle line:84%
But it might be that I offer
the Donkey Kong cereals,

00:53:16.430 --> 00:53:20.940 align:middle line:84%
and my competitor offers the
Donkey Kong Junior cereals.

00:53:20.940 --> 00:53:24.870 align:middle line:84%
And advertising,
kids see the ads.

00:53:24.870 --> 00:53:26.870 align:middle line:84%
And, of course,
in all the markets

00:53:26.870 --> 00:53:29.510 align:middle line:84%
where I'm competing
with Donkey Kong Junior,

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:31.350 align:middle line:90%
I have to compete much harder.

00:53:31.350 --> 00:53:33.320 align:middle line:84%
And those brand dummies
would pick that up.

00:53:33.320 --> 00:53:39.240 align:middle line:84%
So they capture not just the
way that firms are responding

00:53:39.240 --> 00:53:41.340 align:middle line:90%
to observed product attributes.

00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:44.070 align:middle line:84%
They could potentially
also capture other things

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:46.450 align:middle line:84%
that the researcher does
not directly observe.

00:53:46.450 --> 00:53:49.870 align:middle line:90%
So that can be useful.

00:53:49.870 --> 00:53:53.560 align:middle line:84%
But of course, you cannot
include those and also directly

00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:54.650 align:middle line:90%
control for them.

00:53:54.650 --> 00:53:57.310 align:middle line:84%
So that's why he
uses here, these

00:53:57.310 --> 00:53:59.630 align:middle line:84%
are indicating the
Hausman instruments.

00:53:59.630 --> 00:54:02.170 align:middle line:84%
So average prices
in other markets,

00:54:02.170 --> 00:54:04.190 align:middle line:84%
as well as these cost
side instruments.

00:54:04.190 --> 00:54:06.820 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look at
these specifications here,

00:54:06.820 --> 00:54:08.790 align:middle line:84%
you get even more negative
price coefficient.

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:11.140 align:middle line:84%
I think starting
somewhere here, he

00:54:11.140 --> 00:54:14.200 align:middle line:84%
finds no longer that
there are any elasticities

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:17.471 align:middle line:84%
on the inelastic
portion of demand.

00:54:17.471 --> 00:54:20.840 align:middle line:84%
So I think, again, this
is something useful.

00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:25.240 align:middle line:84%
It gives the reader a sense of
what is driving your estimates,

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:28.270 align:middle line:84%
and it gives you a sense of
what is driving your estimates.

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:34.560 align:middle line:84%
And that's producing these
tables can be very useful.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:38.240 align:middle line:90%


00:54:38.240 --> 00:54:43.440 align:middle line:84%
So let me say something
briefly about domains table

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:46.290 align:middle line:90%
of the main specification.

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:49.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, these are the estimates
from the BLP model,

00:54:49.530 --> 00:54:52.320 align:middle line:90%
what he calls the full model.

00:54:52.320 --> 00:54:56.370 align:middle line:84%
So here is a similar
observation as before.

00:54:56.370 --> 00:54:59.340 align:middle line:84%
Somebody was disappointed that
people didn't like mushiness

00:54:59.340 --> 00:55:00.930 align:middle line:90%
on average.

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:02.850 align:middle line:84%
I was also a bit
disappointed by that

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:04.650 align:middle line:84%
because I also
like mushy cereals,

00:55:04.650 --> 00:55:08.860 align:middle line:84%
but it seems like the top 15%
in the distribution of that.

00:55:08.860 --> 00:55:12.870 align:middle line:84%
So that's actually somewhat
interesting story here

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:15.460 align:middle line:84%
that he had to
actually measure that.

00:55:15.460 --> 00:55:18.600 align:middle line:84%
And it was one of his
classmates, Sandy Black,

00:55:18.600 --> 00:55:24.600 align:middle line:84%
who suggested including
this as a characteristic.

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:29.340 align:middle line:84%
But again, the point here--
the point that is important--

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:34.050 align:middle line:84%
is that you see here the
demographic effects on this

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:35.020 align:middle line:90%
are large.

00:55:35.020 --> 00:55:38.220 align:middle line:84%
And depending in which
demographic you are,

00:55:38.220 --> 00:55:39.870 align:middle line:90%
you actually value--

00:55:39.870 --> 00:55:42.510 align:middle line:84%
the sign on this
attribute flips.

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:50.840 align:middle line:84%
And again, shows that people
can value different attributes

00:55:50.840 --> 00:55:51.710 align:middle line:90%
differently.

00:55:51.710 --> 00:55:55.460 align:middle line:84%
The one substantive point
that I want to make here

00:55:55.460 --> 00:56:00.570 align:middle line:84%
is that if you look at what
drives the random coefficients,

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:04.380 align:middle line:84%
you see that many of the
demographic interaction effects

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:07.690 align:middle line:90%
are significant.

00:56:07.690 --> 00:56:10.730 align:middle line:84%
But many of the standard
deviations which capture this

00:56:10.730 --> 00:56:15.590 align:middle line:84%
unobserved term in the
random coefficient-- which,

00:56:15.590 --> 00:56:18.440 align:middle line:84%
in the BLP random coefficients,
that was the only source that

00:56:18.440 --> 00:56:19.770 align:middle line:90%
led to variation--

00:56:19.770 --> 00:56:22.740 align:middle line:90%
are insignificant.

00:56:22.740 --> 00:56:27.960 align:middle line:84%
And so this suggests that
much of this variation,

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:31.080 align:middle line:84%
which was completely
unobserved variation in BLP,

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:35.280 align:middle line:84%
is, in fact, in this market,
driven by demographics.

00:56:35.280 --> 00:56:37.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'll let you be
the judge whether you

00:56:37.170 --> 00:56:40.260 align:middle line:84%
find these interaction
effects convincing.

00:56:40.260 --> 00:56:45.480 align:middle line:84%
It seems like higher income
people like mushy cereals more.

00:56:45.480 --> 00:56:47.770 align:middle line:84%
I don't have very
strong priors on that.

00:56:47.770 --> 00:56:52.840 align:middle line:90%


00:56:52.840 --> 00:56:59.530 align:middle line:84%
So what does he get out of
this at the end of the day?

00:56:59.530 --> 00:57:05.500 align:middle line:84%
So let's focus here on his
preferred BLP specification.

00:57:05.500 --> 00:57:08.920 align:middle line:84%
So to remind you, the
exercise that he engages in

00:57:08.920 --> 00:57:14.830 align:middle line:84%
is I see some mark ups in
accounting data from the census

00:57:14.830 --> 00:57:16.245 align:middle line:90%
of manufacturing.

00:57:16.245 --> 00:57:19.180 align:middle line:84%
And for a second, let's say
these are the true markups

00:57:19.180 --> 00:57:21.250 align:middle line:84%
and they do not have
the kinds of issues

00:57:21.250 --> 00:57:25.570 align:middle line:84%
that we talked about
last week that they

00:57:25.570 --> 00:57:29.500 align:middle line:84%
don't account for things like
opportunity cost and so on.

00:57:29.500 --> 00:57:32.740 align:middle line:84%
So these are not necessarily
the correct economic costs

00:57:32.740 --> 00:57:35.560 align:middle line:84%
that are measured in
this accounting data.

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:38.450 align:middle line:84%
But let's say for a moment
these are the true markups.

00:57:38.450 --> 00:57:42.850 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is I
estimate my demand model,

00:57:42.850 --> 00:57:44.290 align:middle line:84%
use different
conduct assumptions,

00:57:44.290 --> 00:57:47.830 align:middle line:84%
and then see which conduct
assumption replicates

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:49.720 align:middle line:90%
the true markups in the data.

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:53.670 align:middle line:84%
That's the essence
of this exercise.

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:57.820 align:middle line:84%
And so what he finds, so he has
this range of markup estimates

00:57:57.820 --> 00:58:00.220 align:middle line:84%
depending on whether or
not you include advertising

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:05.090 align:middle line:84%
in marginal cost, which
ranges from 31% to 46%,

00:58:05.090 --> 00:58:10.930 align:middle line:84%
he finds that you can soundly
reject this collusive model

00:58:10.930 --> 00:58:15.160 align:middle line:84%
where, for the 25 brands that
he includes in his estimation,

00:58:15.160 --> 00:58:16.920 align:middle line:90%
firms jointly maximize profits.

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:19.988 align:middle line:90%


00:58:19.988 --> 00:58:23.810 align:middle line:84%
And he cannot reject any
of the alternative models,

00:58:23.810 --> 00:58:25.790 align:middle line:90%
which is either firms--

00:58:25.790 --> 00:58:29.870 align:middle line:84%
or that each brand
competes separately.

00:58:29.870 --> 00:58:32.480 align:middle line:84%
Prices are set at
the brand level

00:58:32.480 --> 00:58:39.330 align:middle line:84%
without internalizing these
cannibalization effects as well

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:41.830 align:middle line:84%
as the current ownership, where
he uses the actual ownership

00:58:41.830 --> 00:58:42.830 align:middle line:90%
matrix that he observes.

00:58:42.830 --> 00:58:46.710 align:middle line:90%


00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:50.160 align:middle line:84%
One interesting thing here
is that with the logit model,

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.770 align:middle line:84%
you get, in general,
much, much lower markups.

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:55.780 align:middle line:84%
And you would reach a
very different conclusion.

00:58:55.780 --> 00:58:58.990 align:middle line:84%
You wouldn't reject
the collusive model.

00:58:58.990 --> 00:59:02.370 align:middle line:84%
So this, again, shows you that
for this substantive question,

00:59:02.370 --> 00:59:05.040 align:middle line:84%
which is, are firms in
this market colluding?

00:59:05.040 --> 00:59:09.600 align:middle line:84%
It seems to be important to
account for these more realistic

00:59:09.600 --> 00:59:11.790 align:middle line:84%
substitution patterns where
different people value

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:13.240 align:middle line:90%
product attributes differently.

00:59:13.240 --> 00:59:21.220 align:middle line:90%


00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:25.170 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is a well
written and explained paper.

00:59:25.170 --> 00:59:28.980 align:middle line:84%
In part, I asked you to
read it because it provides

00:59:28.980 --> 00:59:32.490 align:middle line:84%
a good explanation
of the BLP model

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:34.170 align:middle line:84%
without many of the
technical aspects

00:59:34.170 --> 00:59:37.800 align:middle line:84%
that we will discuss
down the line,

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:45.530 align:middle line:84%
and you know, in part, Aviv Nevo
is also known in the profession

00:59:45.530 --> 00:59:47.392 align:middle line:84%
because he has written
this practitioner's

00:59:47.392 --> 00:59:49.100 align:middle line:84%
guide at the very
beginning that actually

00:59:49.100 --> 00:59:51.900 align:middle line:84%
taught graduate students how
to estimate these models.

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:57.170 align:middle line:84%
And he put his code out there,
so it was very useful for people

00:59:57.170 --> 00:59:58.780 align:middle line:90%
to understand what is going on.

00:59:58.780 --> 01:00:01.430 align:middle line:90%


01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:05.420 align:middle line:84%
This is, in terms of thinking
about picking an industry

01:00:05.420 --> 01:00:07.850 align:middle line:84%
and the right model for
this industry, what I really

01:00:07.850 --> 01:00:12.050 align:middle line:84%
like about the paper is it
would be a bit crazy to estimate

01:00:12.050 --> 01:00:14.030 align:middle line:84%
a model for this industry
without accounting

01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:16.220 align:middle line:84%
for these multi-product
considerations.

01:00:16.220 --> 01:00:21.830 align:middle line:84%
At least as a prior, you'd think
that it would matter a lot.

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:23.870 align:middle line:84%
You have four large
firms that account

01:00:23.870 --> 01:00:25.820 align:middle line:84%
for almost the entire
market, and, there are

01:00:25.820 --> 01:00:27.920 align:middle line:90%
hundreds of different products.

01:00:27.920 --> 01:00:33.260 align:middle line:84%
So you have to do
something about that.

01:00:33.260 --> 01:00:37.670 align:middle line:84%
Collusion is, obviously, one
of the important topics in IO.

01:00:37.670 --> 01:00:41.240 align:middle line:84%
So it might not speak to
your preferred industry,

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:44.570 align:middle line:84%
but it speaks to a
substantive economic question.

01:00:44.570 --> 01:00:47.030 align:middle line:84%
You could bicker that
advertising is not

01:00:47.030 --> 01:00:48.030 align:middle line:90%
a strategic variable.

01:00:48.030 --> 01:00:50.390 align:middle line:84%
We know, actually, from
the paper that advertising

01:00:50.390 --> 01:00:52.760 align:middle line:84%
is very important
in this market,

01:00:52.760 --> 01:00:55.050 align:middle line:84%
and I imagine it's
particularly important

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:56.300 align:middle line:90%
when you introduce new brands.

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:58.550 align:middle line:84%
You want to make
them known to kids

01:00:58.550 --> 01:01:01.640 align:middle line:90%
that there's a new Donkey Kong--

01:01:01.640 --> 01:01:03.170 align:middle line:90%
the new Donkey Kong cereal.

01:01:03.170 --> 01:01:10.200 align:middle line:84%
So I guess another potential
criticism is would firms

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:13.740 align:middle line:84%
actually be able to implement
this, from their point,

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:16.170 align:middle line:84%
first best solution
of full collusion

01:01:16.170 --> 01:01:18.360 align:middle line:84%
across all the
different products?

01:01:18.360 --> 01:01:21.368 align:middle line:84%
And so is that the
right comparison point?

01:01:21.368 --> 01:01:23.410 align:middle line:84%
And then, as I already
said, the testing approach

01:01:23.410 --> 01:01:26.000 align:middle line:84%
is predicated on observing
the right accounting cost.

01:01:26.000 --> 01:01:28.810 align:middle line:90%
And that's not something that--

01:01:28.810 --> 01:01:31.360 align:middle line:84%
you know, once you use
that as a benchmark,

01:01:31.360 --> 01:01:34.700 align:middle line:84%
you can obviously no longer
say anything about that.

01:01:34.700 --> 01:01:38.180 align:middle line:90%


01:01:38.180 --> 01:01:40.770 align:middle line:90%
Any other comments on the paper?

01:01:40.770 --> 01:01:41.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:42.905 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in some
part of the paper,

01:01:42.905 --> 01:01:46.950 align:middle line:84%
he argues that one shortcoming
of the BLP literature

01:01:46.950 --> 01:01:51.750 align:middle line:84%
is that you take observed
product characteristics as given

01:01:51.750 --> 01:01:54.570 align:middle line:84%
or as exogenous, and then
you should internalize those

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:58.590 align:middle line:84%
because firms actually
choose which characteristics

01:01:58.590 --> 01:02:00.335 align:middle line:90%
will [INAUDIBLE] target.

01:02:00.335 --> 01:02:02.235 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:02.235 --> 01:02:03.660 align:middle line:90%


01:02:03.660 --> 01:02:07.680 align:middle line:84%
So in his model,
is he implicitly

01:02:07.680 --> 01:02:12.270 align:middle line:84%
allowing for firms to choose
certain product characteristics?

01:02:12.270 --> 01:02:14.640 align:middle line:84%
Because I don't think
he's improving it.

01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:17.850 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: No, he's
not doing that.

01:02:17.850 --> 01:02:19.530 align:middle line:84%
The only endogenous
characteristic

01:02:19.530 --> 01:02:21.040 align:middle line:90%
is price in this case.

01:02:21.040 --> 01:02:23.520 align:middle line:84%
And again, advertising would
be a very good candidate

01:02:23.520 --> 01:02:26.580 align:middle line:84%
for that because that's
something that we potentially

01:02:26.580 --> 01:02:29.220 align:middle line:84%
think varies from, let's
say, quarter to quarter,

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:30.963 align:middle line:90%
city to city.

01:02:30.963 --> 01:02:34.680 align:middle line:84%
And there are papers that
look at advertising effects,

01:02:34.680 --> 01:02:39.100 align:middle line:84%
and do use advertising as
an endogenous variable.

01:02:39.100 --> 01:02:40.750 align:middle line:84%
Then, of course,
you have to think

01:02:40.750 --> 01:02:42.610 align:middle line:90%
about additional instruments.

01:02:42.610 --> 01:02:47.460 align:middle line:90%


01:02:47.460 --> 01:02:51.310 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, that's not something
that he tackles in this paper.

01:02:51.310 --> 01:02:56.960 align:middle line:90%


01:02:56.960 --> 01:03:03.690 align:middle line:84%
So let me use the last 20
minutes to talk about something

01:03:03.690 --> 01:03:04.190 align:middle line:90%
that--

01:03:04.190 --> 01:03:09.080 align:middle line:84%
we're staying in this industry,
but moving to a new topic.

01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:12.410 align:middle line:84%
Which it's a fairly
recent debate that

01:03:12.410 --> 01:03:16.200 align:middle line:84%
came up in IO, which is called
the common ownership hypothesis.

01:03:16.200 --> 01:03:20.840 align:middle line:84%
It's something that's fairly
controversial and hotly debated

01:03:20.840 --> 01:03:27.320 align:middle line:84%
with strong proponents on
either side of the argument.

01:03:27.320 --> 01:03:29.540 align:middle line:90%
So what's the idea?

01:03:29.540 --> 01:03:32.630 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that we
have seen this big trend

01:03:32.630 --> 01:03:35.900 align:middle line:84%
towards diversified
investments where people

01:03:35.900 --> 01:03:39.800 align:middle line:84%
hold large portfolios of
the entire, let's say, S&P

01:03:39.800 --> 01:03:43.920 align:middle line:90%
500, or stock indices.

01:03:43.920 --> 01:03:46.380 align:middle line:84%
And typically,
they purchase those

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:48.870 align:middle line:84%
through large institutional
investors like Vanguard,

01:03:48.870 --> 01:03:51.690 align:middle line:90%
BlackRock, and Fidelity.

01:03:51.690 --> 01:03:55.770 align:middle line:84%
And so a lot of people
use those to save

01:03:55.770 --> 01:03:59.300 align:middle line:90%
for retirement and so on.

01:03:59.300 --> 01:04:04.885 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is that these
owners, they might hold--

01:04:04.885 --> 01:04:06.260 align:middle line:84%
or these institutional
investors,

01:04:06.260 --> 01:04:08.240 align:middle line:84%
they might hold
significant shares

01:04:08.240 --> 01:04:10.160 align:middle line:84%
in a number of
different companies

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:13.170 align:middle line:84%
that are actually
competing with each other.

01:04:13.170 --> 01:04:16.650 align:middle line:84%
So here is an example of
the US airline market.

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:20.340 align:middle line:84%
You have American Airlines,
Delta, Southwest and United,

01:04:20.340 --> 01:04:22.260 align:middle line:90%
the major airlines.

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:24.450 align:middle line:84%
And you look, who are
the biggest shareholders

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:27.610 align:middle line:90%
of these companies?

01:04:27.610 --> 01:04:30.450 align:middle line:84%
Vanguard holds 6% of
American Airlines.

01:04:30.450 --> 01:04:32.370 align:middle line:90%
It holds 6% of Delta Airlines.

01:04:32.370 --> 01:04:38.070 align:middle line:84%
It holds 6% of Southwest,
and 7% of United Airlines.

01:04:38.070 --> 01:04:40.560 align:middle line:84%
And so the idea here,
this hypothesis,

01:04:40.560 --> 01:04:45.900 align:middle line:84%
is that if I'm holding stocks
in a bunch of companies

01:04:45.900 --> 01:04:49.500 align:middle line:84%
that should be competing with
each other, who says that.

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:51.440 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't be very
interested in making

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:55.520 align:middle line:84%
these companies compete a little
less hard and raise my profits?

01:04:55.520 --> 01:05:02.580 align:middle line:84%
Moving the incentives closer
to monopoly incentives.

01:05:02.580 --> 01:05:05.820 align:middle line:84%
So this is something
that people had

01:05:05.820 --> 01:05:08.640 align:middle line:84%
thought about in literature
at the intersection

01:05:08.640 --> 01:05:10.170 align:middle line:90%
of corporate finance and IO.

01:05:10.170 --> 01:05:12.180 align:middle line:84%
There are some theory
papers that I'll

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:15.930 align:middle line:84%
talk briefly about just to
clarify what this hypothesis is

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:18.990 align:middle line:84%
about, but it wasn't
really until very recently

01:05:18.990 --> 01:05:21.850 align:middle line:84%
that this was-- that people
paid a lot of attention to this.

01:05:21.850 --> 01:05:24.840 align:middle line:84%
And of course, in part, this
attention is driven by the fact

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:28.380 align:middle line:84%
that we have, now, these large
diversified investments that

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:30.220 align:middle line:90%
have been trending up.

01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:34.870 align:middle line:84%
So there was a series
of papers in 2016, 2018,

01:05:34.870 --> 01:05:37.050 align:middle line:84%
now there are many
more, that document

01:05:37.050 --> 01:05:42.030 align:middle line:84%
that the growth of large,
diversified common owners

01:05:42.030 --> 01:05:44.760 align:middle line:84%
led to increase in prices
in various industries,

01:05:44.760 --> 01:05:48.510 align:middle line:84%
for instance, for banking
services or for airlines.

01:05:48.510 --> 01:05:52.680 align:middle line:84%
So these were
actually people that--

01:05:52.680 --> 01:05:54.840 align:middle line:84%
this was first
researched in finance.

01:05:54.840 --> 01:05:58.500 align:middle line:84%
And so it was a little
bit awkward for IO people.

01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:00.150 align:middle line:84%
Could we have
possibly missed this,

01:06:00.150 --> 01:06:04.990 align:middle line:84%
that this big structural force
that led the whole economy

01:06:04.990 --> 01:06:08.800 align:middle line:90%
to become less competitive?

01:06:08.800 --> 01:06:12.157 align:middle line:84%
And do we really think
that the price that we

01:06:12.157 --> 01:06:13.990 align:middle line:84%
pay for these cheap,
diversified investments

01:06:13.990 --> 01:06:16.360 align:middle line:84%
is that monopoly
power as a whole

01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:20.560 align:middle line:90%
has increased in the US economy?

01:06:20.560 --> 01:06:23.420 align:middle line:84%
So these papers
suggest that it did.

01:06:23.420 --> 01:06:27.868 align:middle line:84%
Now, they use an approach which
I think Glenn has talked about,

01:06:27.868 --> 01:06:30.160 align:middle line:84%
which is a structure conduct
performance approach where

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:33.800 align:middle line:84%
you essentially regress prices
on measures of concentration.

01:06:33.800 --> 01:06:38.950 align:middle line:84%
And we know that this
has well-known issues,

01:06:38.950 --> 01:06:42.550 align:middle line:84%
in part because these
concentration measures are

01:06:42.550 --> 01:06:46.350 align:middle line:84%
themselves driven by quantities
which are endogenous.

01:06:46.350 --> 01:06:51.670 align:middle line:84%
So today, we're going to
talk about one paper that

01:06:51.670 --> 01:06:53.950 align:middle line:84%
tries to do this in
a structural way,

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:56.440 align:middle line:84%
and a companion paper
to that that documents

01:06:56.440 --> 01:07:00.400 align:middle line:84%
a little bit to what extent this
is an actual issue in the US

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:01.820 align:middle line:90%
economy.

01:07:01.820 --> 01:07:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So just very briefly,
what is this idea here?

01:07:07.250 --> 01:07:09.860 align:middle line:84%
Formally, the common
ownership hypothesis,

01:07:09.860 --> 01:07:13.190 align:middle line:84%
according to this paper
by Rotemberg-- and here,

01:07:13.190 --> 01:07:16.430 align:middle line:84%
I'm using the notation
of O'Brien and Salop--

01:07:16.430 --> 01:07:20.420 align:middle line:84%
is the idea that we
have an investor, S,

01:07:20.420 --> 01:07:25.550 align:middle line:84%
or different investors,
that own a portfolio

01:07:25.550 --> 01:07:28.592 align:middle line:84%
of shares in
different companies.

01:07:28.592 --> 01:07:30.800 align:middle line:84%
And according to the amount
of shares that they hold,

01:07:30.800 --> 01:07:33.350 align:middle line:84%
they are entitled to some
fraction of the profits of each

01:07:33.350 --> 01:07:35.080 align:middle line:90%
of the firms that they hold.

01:07:35.080 --> 01:07:38.360 align:middle line:90%


01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:45.870 align:middle line:84%
And you have firms
that, in this model,

01:07:45.870 --> 01:07:49.220 align:middle line:84%
maximizing the value
of the shareholders.

01:07:49.220 --> 01:07:52.150 align:middle line:90%
So how do they do that?

01:07:52.150 --> 01:07:54.045 align:middle line:84%
By virtue of having
different portfolios,

01:07:54.045 --> 01:07:55.420 align:middle line:84%
different shareholders
might have

01:07:55.420 --> 01:07:56.628 align:middle line:90%
slightly different interests.

01:07:56.628 --> 01:07:58.600 align:middle line:84%
Their interests might
not be fully aligned.

01:07:58.600 --> 01:08:00.790 align:middle line:84%
So in this model, the
way this is handled

01:08:00.790 --> 01:08:04.193 align:middle line:84%
is that we're saying the firm
places different weights on what

01:08:04.193 --> 01:08:05.360 align:middle line:90%
different shareholders want.

01:08:05.360 --> 01:08:07.540 align:middle line:90%
So these are pareto weights.

01:08:07.540 --> 01:08:11.110 align:middle line:84%
So you can write the
firm's objective function,

01:08:11.110 --> 01:08:12.850 align:middle line:84%
which is a function
of its own prices

01:08:12.850 --> 01:08:18.692 align:middle line:84%
and other prices in the market,
as a weighted sum over what

01:08:18.692 --> 01:08:20.025 align:middle line:90%
its different shareholders want.

01:08:20.025 --> 01:08:23.330 align:middle line:90%


01:08:23.330 --> 01:08:29.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you plug this in here
using this formula from above,

01:08:29.830 --> 01:08:35.380 align:middle line:84%
the shareholder objective
function, the portfolio,

01:08:35.380 --> 01:08:38.590 align:middle line:84%
you find that you're
suddenly in a situation

01:08:38.590 --> 01:08:42.040 align:middle line:84%
where this firm,
F, might be caring

01:08:42.040 --> 01:08:46.000 align:middle line:84%
about the profits of some
firm, G, in the market

01:08:46.000 --> 01:08:48.800 align:middle line:84%
through the incentives
of its shareholders.

01:08:48.800 --> 01:08:51.670 align:middle line:90%


01:08:51.670 --> 01:08:53.330 align:middle line:90%
So these are the pareto weights.

01:08:53.330 --> 01:08:58.500 align:middle line:84%
These are these cash flow
shares that the investor, S,

01:08:58.500 --> 01:09:03.270 align:middle line:90%
is entitled to terms of firm G.

01:09:03.270 --> 01:09:05.240 align:middle line:90%
So you can rewrite this.

01:09:05.240 --> 01:09:11.660 align:middle line:84%
You can do some algebra and
normalize the share of firm F

01:09:11.660 --> 01:09:14.300 align:middle line:84%
to 1, and can write
the objective function

01:09:14.300 --> 01:09:19.910 align:middle line:84%
of firm F as its own
profits plus a weighted sum

01:09:19.910 --> 01:09:23.675 align:middle line:90%
of other firms in the market.

01:09:23.675 --> 01:09:27.260 align:middle line:90%


01:09:27.260 --> 01:09:30.109 align:middle line:84%
And so we call these
profit weights here kappa.

01:09:30.109 --> 01:09:34.970 align:middle line:84%
They're a function of these
pareto weights and these betas.

01:09:34.970 --> 01:09:37.100 align:middle line:84%
So just to link
this back to what

01:09:37.100 --> 01:09:40.450 align:middle line:84%
I discussed at the very
beginning of class today,

01:09:40.450 --> 01:09:44.770 align:middle line:84%
this should remind
you of something

01:09:44.770 --> 01:09:46.840 align:middle line:84%
that we have seen
on the first slide.

01:09:46.840 --> 01:09:49.390 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have a
firm that's maximizing

01:09:49.390 --> 01:09:52.779 align:middle line:84%
its own profits plus some
weighted sum of other firm's

01:09:52.779 --> 01:09:54.200 align:middle line:90%
profits.

01:09:54.200 --> 01:09:58.040 align:middle line:84%
This is very similar to this
idea of a multi-product firm

01:09:58.040 --> 01:10:03.110 align:middle line:84%
that owns different brands, and
will take into account that if I

01:10:03.110 --> 01:10:04.850 align:middle line:84%
lower my price on
one of my brands,

01:10:04.850 --> 01:10:06.290 align:middle line:84%
that might potentially
cannibalize

01:10:06.290 --> 01:10:14.110 align:middle line:84%
one of my other brands and
divert some market share.

01:10:14.110 --> 01:10:22.110 align:middle line:84%
So here, you get a very similar
first order condition for firm J

01:10:22.110 --> 01:10:26.340 align:middle line:84%
that places these profit
rates that come, again,

01:10:26.340 --> 01:10:32.430 align:middle line:84%
through that are a function
of the shareholder holdings

01:10:32.430 --> 01:10:36.450 align:middle line:90%
on other firms' profits.

01:10:36.450 --> 01:10:40.130 align:middle line:84%
So I hope this
connection is clear.

01:10:40.130 --> 01:10:42.860 align:middle line:90%


01:10:42.860 --> 01:10:48.350 align:middle line:84%
So what can we actually say
about those profit weights?

01:10:48.350 --> 01:10:52.940 align:middle line:84%
So this is what this companion
paper by Backus, Conlon,

01:10:52.940 --> 01:10:54.530 align:middle line:90%
and Sinkinson does.

01:10:54.530 --> 01:10:56.210 align:middle line:84%
The first document,
is it, indeed,

01:10:56.210 --> 01:11:00.290 align:middle line:84%
true that, just as a pure
matter of accounting,

01:11:00.290 --> 01:11:03.230 align:middle line:84%
that firms are placing,
or have these incentives

01:11:03.230 --> 01:11:08.750 align:middle line:84%
to place more weight on what
their competitors are doing,

01:11:08.750 --> 01:11:12.526 align:middle line:84%
or any other random
firm in the market?

01:11:12.526 --> 01:11:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So first, if you look at
the time series of what

01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:18.883 align:middle line:84%
fraction of the typical
firm is actually

01:11:18.883 --> 01:11:21.050 align:middle line:84%
held by one of these large
institutional investors--

01:11:21.050 --> 01:11:23.360 align:middle line:84%
BlackRock, Vanguard,
and State Street--

01:11:23.360 --> 01:11:28.364 align:middle line:84%
we find that this did,
indeed, go up a lot.

01:11:28.364 --> 01:11:32.642 align:middle line:84%
And, I mean, this is
actually not just--

01:11:32.642 --> 01:11:34.350 align:middle line:84%
this trend towards
diversified investment

01:11:34.350 --> 01:11:36.880 align:middle line:84%
is not just driven by these
institutional investors.

01:11:36.880 --> 01:11:40.590 align:middle line:84%
This was another result in the
paper was already going on even

01:11:40.590 --> 01:11:41.930 align:middle line:90%
before that.

01:11:41.930 --> 01:11:45.930 align:middle line:84%
So they use some
data from SEC filings

01:11:45.930 --> 01:11:52.490 align:middle line:84%
that large investment managers
have to submit to document this.

01:11:52.490 --> 01:11:55.310 align:middle line:84%
So then, they compute
these profit weights

01:11:55.310 --> 01:11:59.900 align:middle line:84%
that I've just shown you that
you can derive from the firm's

01:11:59.900 --> 01:12:01.880 align:middle line:84%
profit maximization
problem, taking

01:12:01.880 --> 01:12:05.075 align:middle line:84%
into account the incentives
of the shareholders.

01:12:05.075 --> 01:12:07.057 align:middle line:84%
So the one issue that
they have here is they

01:12:07.057 --> 01:12:08.640 align:middle line:84%
don't actually see
this pareto weight.

01:12:08.640 --> 01:12:11.057 align:middle line:84%
So they make some assumption
that these pareto weights are

01:12:11.057 --> 01:12:14.860 align:middle line:84%
a function of these
shareholder weights beta,

01:12:14.860 --> 01:12:19.400 align:middle line:84%
and so they show how
this time series look

01:12:19.400 --> 01:12:22.410 align:middle line:90%
for different functional forms.

01:12:22.410 --> 01:12:23.930 align:middle line:90%
But the general message is--

01:12:23.930 --> 01:12:25.910 align:middle line:84%
and you can also break
this down by industry--

01:12:25.910 --> 01:12:29.810 align:middle line:84%
that if you take
a firm in the US,

01:12:29.810 --> 01:12:32.450 align:middle line:84%
it's a publicly traded
firm in the US economy,

01:12:32.450 --> 01:12:35.330 align:middle line:84%
and ask how much weight does
that firm place on the profits

01:12:35.330 --> 01:12:38.660 align:middle line:84%
according to these
diversified investments

01:12:38.660 --> 01:12:41.470 align:middle line:84%
on any other random firm, that
this has gone up over time.

01:12:41.470 --> 01:12:45.162 align:middle line:90%


01:12:45.162 --> 01:12:49.160 align:middle line:84%
So this suggests that in
principle, these kinds

01:12:49.160 --> 01:12:52.330 align:middle line:90%
of incentives could be at play.

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:56.050 align:middle line:90%


01:12:56.050 --> 01:12:57.165 align:middle line:90%
Any questions here?

01:12:57.165 --> 01:12:59.653 align:middle line:90%


01:12:59.653 --> 01:13:02.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there any sense of
how sensitive these measurements

01:13:02.320 --> 01:13:03.403 align:middle line:90%
are to the pareto weights?

01:13:03.403 --> 01:13:06.940 align:middle line:84%
Like were there different
specifications tested,

01:13:06.940 --> 01:13:08.140 align:middle line:90%
or [INAUDIBLE]?

01:13:08.140 --> 01:13:10.820 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: This trend is
not very sensitive to that.

01:13:10.820 --> 01:13:13.840 align:middle line:84%
The levels are-- I mean, in
the paper, they show you,

01:13:13.840 --> 01:13:15.880 align:middle line:84%
I think, three different
types of weights.

01:13:15.880 --> 01:13:19.360 align:middle line:84%
But the general message is
that this has been upwards

01:13:19.360 --> 01:13:21.070 align:middle line:90%
trending regardless of--

01:13:21.070 --> 01:13:22.930 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you can then,
of course, argue,

01:13:22.930 --> 01:13:26.800 align:middle line:84%
is that an exhaustive search
over the potential specification

01:13:26.800 --> 01:13:27.598 align:middle line:90%
that you could use?

01:13:27.598 --> 01:13:28.098 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:28.098 --> 01:13:34.740 align:middle line:90%


01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:37.450 align:middle line:84%
So coming back to
the cereals market.

01:13:37.450 --> 01:13:39.180 align:middle line:84%
So again, what
I've just shown you

01:13:39.180 --> 01:13:43.320 align:middle line:84%
is from this descriptive
paper is that they document

01:13:43.320 --> 01:13:49.757 align:middle line:84%
that these incentives, the
fact that firms may internalize

01:13:49.757 --> 01:13:51.840 align:middle line:84%
profits of other firms
that they're competing with

01:13:51.840 --> 01:13:54.990 align:middle line:84%
could potentially be an issue
based on the time series

01:13:54.990 --> 01:13:58.800 align:middle line:84%
that we observe because
of this trend towards more

01:13:58.800 --> 01:14:00.120 align:middle line:90%
diversified investment.

01:14:00.120 --> 01:14:03.660 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
breakfast cereal

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:05.730 align:middle line:84%
market and the different
producers here,

01:14:05.730 --> 01:14:11.880 align:middle line:84%
you'll find that, indeed,
BlackRock also, in 2016, held

01:14:11.880 --> 01:14:17.670 align:middle line:84%
a large share of General Mills,
of Kellogg, of Quaker Oats,

01:14:17.670 --> 01:14:21.960 align:middle line:84%
and Post brands-- so
all the big producers.

01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:24.300 align:middle line:84%
So in principle, this could
be an issue in this market,

01:14:24.300 --> 01:14:24.800 align:middle line:90%
as well.

01:14:24.800 --> 01:14:30.850 align:middle line:90%


01:14:30.850 --> 01:14:32.780 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what is it
that they're doing?

01:14:32.780 --> 01:14:35.080 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to be--
you know, I'll give you

01:14:35.080 --> 01:14:36.560 align:middle line:90%
the main economic idea here.

01:14:36.560 --> 01:14:41.680 align:middle line:84%
This paper, big part
of the contribution

01:14:41.680 --> 01:14:46.240 align:middle line:84%
is develop a new and efficient
testing procedure for this

01:14:46.240 --> 01:14:50.420 align:middle line:84%
and allowing for
flexible cost function.

01:14:50.420 --> 01:14:53.260 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to talk
about the econometric details,

01:14:53.260 --> 01:14:59.650 align:middle line:84%
but this is very much in the
same spirit as the other papers

01:14:59.650 --> 01:15:01.030 align:middle line:90%
that we have been discussing--

01:15:01.030 --> 01:15:03.370 align:middle line:90%
Nevo's paper on conduct testing.

01:15:03.370 --> 01:15:09.730 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is that with my
demand model in hand and conduct

01:15:09.730 --> 01:15:14.200 align:middle line:84%
assumption, I can invert
my first order conditions

01:15:14.200 --> 01:15:17.170 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the observed
market shares, prices,

01:15:17.170 --> 01:15:18.550 align:middle line:90%
and the demand model.

01:15:18.550 --> 01:15:20.740 align:middle line:84%
Again, the demand
model, of course,

01:15:20.740 --> 01:15:23.500 align:middle line:84%
has some demand parameters,
and can back out

01:15:23.500 --> 01:15:26.445 align:middle line:90%
my effective marginal cost.

01:15:26.445 --> 01:15:29.120 align:middle line:90%


01:15:29.120 --> 01:15:34.700 align:middle line:84%
At the same time, I may
be willing to also assume

01:15:34.700 --> 01:15:38.480 align:middle line:84%
a functional form for the
marginal cost directly.

01:15:38.480 --> 01:15:42.590 align:middle line:84%
Again, because I know that
the cost of producing cereals

01:15:42.590 --> 01:15:46.200 align:middle line:84%
are driven by the cost of
sugar, wheat, flour, and so on.

01:15:46.200 --> 01:15:49.665 align:middle line:90%


01:15:49.665 --> 01:15:56.670 align:middle line:84%
So what they do is they
basically specify this equation

01:15:56.670 --> 01:16:01.830 align:middle line:84%
here by allowing for a flexible
supply function or marginal cost

01:16:01.830 --> 01:16:02.680 align:middle line:90%
function.

01:16:02.680 --> 01:16:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And based off this, get
additional moment restrictions

01:16:06.270 --> 01:16:09.360 align:middle line:84%
that they use for the
construction of a GMM objective

01:16:09.360 --> 01:16:13.582 align:middle line:84%
function under different
conduct models, M.

01:16:13.582 --> 01:16:15.290 align:middle line:84%
And of course, in
particular, the conduct

01:16:15.290 --> 01:16:17.060 align:middle line:84%
model that they
are interested in

01:16:17.060 --> 01:16:20.900 align:middle line:84%
is the common
ownership hypothesis.

01:16:20.900 --> 01:16:23.870 align:middle line:84%
And that is, again, very
similar to this idea

01:16:23.870 --> 01:16:27.590 align:middle line:84%
that I might be
internalizing the profits not

01:16:27.590 --> 01:16:32.540 align:middle line:84%
of other products that I
hold, but of other firms

01:16:32.540 --> 01:16:35.070 align:middle line:84%
through the incentives of
my diversified investors.

01:16:35.070 --> 01:16:39.020 align:middle line:90%


01:16:39.020 --> 01:16:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here is to
construct this equation

01:16:44.660 --> 01:16:46.290 align:middle line:90%
for different conduct models.

01:16:46.290 --> 01:16:49.220 align:middle line:84%
You get some GMM objective
function out of that.

01:16:49.220 --> 01:16:51.170 align:middle line:84%
And with this GMM
objective function,

01:16:51.170 --> 01:16:53.880 align:middle line:84%
you can construct
a test statistic.

01:16:53.880 --> 01:16:56.450 align:middle line:90%
This here is a test that's--

01:16:56.450 --> 01:16:59.120 align:middle line:84%
it's a non-nested test
by Rivers and Vuong.

01:16:59.120 --> 01:17:02.420 align:middle line:84%
The idea of this test is that
there are two different models

01:17:02.420 --> 01:17:06.350 align:middle line:84%
of conduct, and the null
hypothesis is that both of them

01:17:06.350 --> 01:17:09.940 align:middle line:84%
are equally far
from the true model.

01:17:09.940 --> 01:17:12.750 align:middle line:84%
But you're always testing the
two models against each other.

01:17:12.750 --> 01:17:14.860 align:middle line:84%
So what, at the end of
the day, you can say is,

01:17:14.860 --> 01:17:17.350 align:middle line:84%
is this model better
describing the data

01:17:17.350 --> 01:17:20.320 align:middle line:84%
that I observed in
some other model?

01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:23.020 align:middle line:84%
And so, for instance,
here, based on,

01:17:23.020 --> 01:17:27.940 align:middle line:84%
let's say, common ownership
or own profit maximization,

01:17:27.940 --> 01:17:30.940 align:middle line:84%
if this test statistic
here becomes very large,

01:17:30.940 --> 01:17:35.540 align:middle line:84%
then I'm rejecting model
1 in favor of model 2.

01:17:35.540 --> 01:17:38.180 align:middle line:84%
Because the violation
of the moment

01:17:38.180 --> 01:17:40.590 align:middle line:84%
conditions is much
larger under model 1.

01:17:40.590 --> 01:17:42.300 align:middle line:90%
This is larger.

01:17:42.300 --> 01:17:48.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's shown that this
is asymptotically normal.

01:17:48.510 --> 01:17:53.510 align:middle line:84%
So you can use just
standard criteria

01:17:53.510 --> 01:17:58.610 align:middle line:84%
for the normal distribution to
test with this test statistic.

01:17:58.610 --> 01:18:01.880 align:middle line:84%
So just, again, to link this
back to what we have already

01:18:01.880 --> 01:18:05.120 align:middle line:84%
discussed today, so like Nevo,
they estimated the demand model

01:18:05.120 --> 01:18:06.890 align:middle line:90%
for cereals.

01:18:06.890 --> 01:18:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Do this first order
condition inversion.

01:18:10.160 --> 01:18:15.020 align:middle line:84%
And like Nevo, they test for
different models of conduct

01:18:15.020 --> 01:18:17.060 align:middle line:84%
with a focus on
common ownership.

01:18:17.060 --> 01:18:21.290 align:middle line:84%
But unlike Nevo, what they
do is instead of comparing

01:18:21.290 --> 01:18:23.510 align:middle line:84%
the markups that I get
from this inversion here

01:18:23.510 --> 01:18:26.720 align:middle line:84%
of the first order conditions
directly to observed markups,

01:18:26.720 --> 01:18:29.750 align:middle line:84%
they're seeing whether
the marginal costs

01:18:29.750 --> 01:18:32.420 align:middle line:84%
that they back
out are consistent

01:18:32.420 --> 01:18:37.850 align:middle line:84%
with the functional form that
I imposed on marginal cost.

01:18:37.850 --> 01:18:41.220 align:middle line:84%
And they basically now used
demand side variation--

01:18:41.220 --> 01:18:44.150 align:middle line:84%
so things that move around the
demand side but I exclude it

01:18:44.150 --> 01:18:45.680 align:middle line:90%
from the supply side--

01:18:45.680 --> 01:18:49.820 align:middle line:84%
as overidentifying
restrictions to test this model

01:18:49.820 --> 01:18:52.490 align:middle line:84%
and construct this
test statistic.

01:18:52.490 --> 01:18:56.180 align:middle line:84%
And this allows them
to compare, let's say,

01:18:56.180 --> 01:19:01.380 align:middle line:84%
the common ownership model
against other types of models.

01:19:01.380 --> 01:19:04.800 align:middle line:84%
So let me tell you what
they find from this.

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:07.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's some details
that I haven't touched on,

01:19:07.050 --> 01:19:09.840 align:middle line:84%
which is how do you specify
the supply function,

01:19:09.840 --> 01:19:12.150 align:middle line:84%
and what are the
best instruments?

01:19:12.150 --> 01:19:15.300 align:middle line:84%
Their preferred instrument
are these optimal demand side

01:19:15.300 --> 01:19:15.850 align:middle line:90%
instruments.

01:19:15.850 --> 01:19:18.180 align:middle line:84%
Again, now you want
to say something

01:19:18.180 --> 01:19:21.600 align:middle line:84%
about this flexible supply side,
so you use demand side variation

01:19:21.600 --> 01:19:23.670 align:middle line:90%
which moves around the markup.

01:19:23.670 --> 01:19:27.720 align:middle line:84%
And under the right
model, these are

01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:29.250 align:middle line:84%
excluded instruments--
instruments

01:19:29.250 --> 01:19:30.875 align:middle line:84%
that are excluded
from the supply side.

01:19:30.875 --> 01:19:36.160 align:middle line:90%


01:19:36.160 --> 01:19:39.080 align:middle line:84%
These should be orthogonal
to the variation in omega.

01:19:39.080 --> 01:19:43.390 align:middle line:84%
And so what you find
here is that using

01:19:43.390 --> 01:19:45.250 align:middle line:84%
these optimal
instruments, they soundly

01:19:45.250 --> 01:19:49.210 align:middle line:84%
reject your different
models of common ownership

01:19:49.210 --> 01:19:52.237 align:middle line:90%
against own profit maximization.

01:19:52.237 --> 01:19:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And so there are different
specifications whether or not

01:19:54.570 --> 01:19:59.130 align:middle line:84%
you allow for firms responding
to a lack of these profit rates.

01:19:59.130 --> 01:20:03.150 align:middle line:84%
They also reject
perfect competition

01:20:03.150 --> 01:20:07.670 align:middle line:84%
using these optimal instruments
as well as a full monopoly

01:20:07.670 --> 01:20:09.630 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:20:09.630 --> 01:20:15.810 align:middle line:84%
So it seems that, against what
these time trends suggest,

01:20:15.810 --> 01:20:19.350 align:middle line:84%
that firms do not seem
to be internalizing

01:20:19.350 --> 01:20:21.100 align:middle line:90%
these common ownership effects.

01:20:21.100 --> 01:20:23.550 align:middle line:84%
And instead, the data
is much more consistent

01:20:23.550 --> 01:20:27.320 align:middle line:90%
with own profit maximization.

01:20:27.320 --> 01:20:34.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is, of course,
just a case study.

01:20:34.070 --> 01:20:41.090 align:middle line:84%
So this doesn't speak to the
US economy in its entirety.

01:20:41.090 --> 01:20:45.950 align:middle line:84%
So again, to wrap up, this is
a very interesting hypothesis

01:20:45.950 --> 01:20:48.740 align:middle line:84%
that has been very
hotly debated in IO,

01:20:48.740 --> 01:20:52.480 align:middle line:84%
and people feel very
strongly about it.

01:20:52.480 --> 01:20:56.080 align:middle line:90%


01:20:56.080 --> 01:20:59.680 align:middle line:84%
You could plausibly
conjecture that firms

01:20:59.680 --> 01:21:01.743 align:middle line:84%
behave in this way based
on these time trends

01:21:01.743 --> 01:21:02.660 align:middle line:90%
that we have observed.

01:21:02.660 --> 01:21:08.970 align:middle line:84%
It's not crazy to think that
they somehow internalize this.

01:21:08.970 --> 01:21:11.550 align:middle line:90%
This case study rejects this.

01:21:11.550 --> 01:21:13.170 align:middle line:84%
And more generally,
people have been

01:21:13.170 --> 01:21:15.570 align:middle line:84%
highly skeptical
of this hypothesis,

01:21:15.570 --> 01:21:19.740 align:middle line:84%
in part due to a lack of a
plausible mechanism of how

01:21:19.740 --> 01:21:21.630 align:middle line:90%
these firms would actually--

01:21:21.630 --> 01:21:24.030 align:middle line:84%
how these institutional
investors would actually

01:21:24.030 --> 01:21:29.610 align:middle line:84%
bring the firms to, let's say,
soften competition, or engage

01:21:29.610 --> 01:21:31.560 align:middle line:90%
in less price competition.

01:21:31.560 --> 01:21:37.740 align:middle line:84%
So the mechanism is
still a bit elusive,

01:21:37.740 --> 01:21:40.820 align:middle line:84%
and, to the best
of my knowledge,

01:21:40.820 --> 01:21:42.860 align:middle line:84%
there has been no smoking
gun-- like a story

01:21:42.860 --> 01:21:46.280 align:middle line:84%
where it's clear that
they have done this.

01:21:46.280 --> 01:21:48.920 align:middle line:84%
Some people argue that
managerial incentives

01:21:48.920 --> 01:21:51.880 align:middle line:84%
could be a potential
explanation for this.

01:21:51.880 --> 01:21:55.640 align:middle line:84%
I think this is an exciting
topic that you should make up

01:21:55.640 --> 01:21:57.680 align:middle line:90%
your own mind on.

01:21:57.680 --> 01:22:01.690 align:middle line:90%
And that's all I have for today.

01:22:01.690 --> 01:22:21.000 align:middle line:90%